    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SCO",
    "TotalRecovery Backup Image",
    "3.5",
    "Configuration file created by OMSI The Bus Simulator, an omnibus driving simulator; contains configuration data for scenery objects that appear during gameplay, such as trees and buildings; can be customized; similar to other OMSI configuration files like.HUM,.SLI,.BUS, and.OVH files. The SCO file is made up of lines of data following a keyword (whether its for a script or description). The format looks like this: [keyword] 1 2 3 OMSI will only read lines of data that are in this format. Windows MR Software OMSI The Bus Simulator 2. Backup file created by TotalRecovery, a system backup and recovery application; stores a disk image of a disk partition or the whole hard drive; can be used for restoring the computer system state exactly as it was saved. SCO files can be converted to VMWare.VMDK disk images in TotalRecovery. NOTE: SCO disk images are also created by Total Backup Recovery Server, a Windows server edition of the software. Windows FarStone TotalRecovery. FarStone Total Backup Recovery Server.",
    "Windows MR Software OMSI The Bus Simulator 2. Windows FarStone TotalRecovery. FarStone Total Backup Recovery Server.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MRIMG",
    "Macrium Reflect Disk Image",
    "3.5",
    "Disk image created by Macrium Reflect, a program used to create Windows hard drive images; contains all files and folders from a disk in a single file; used for disk duplication or to create backups for performing system restores. Windows Paramount Macrium Reflect.",
    "Windows Paramount Macrium Reflect.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MD1",
    "GEAR CD Disc Image",
    "3.5",
    "An MD1 file is a CD-ROM Mode 1 (MD1) disc image created by GEAR Software GEAR PRO disc-burning software. It contains a copy of a CD saved in the Mode 1 layout, which includes tracks subdivided into blocks containing header information, and 2048 bytes of data per sector. GEAR PRO users typically save data CD images as MD1 files. GEAR PRO allows you to manage CD (audio and data), DVD, and Blu-ray images, which includes burning and duplicating discs. When extracting a disc image from a CD, you can save it as an MD1 file. The MD1 format supports any CD-ROM Mode 1 compatible CD image, which includes ISO/Joliet, ISO, ISO/UDF, UDF, Apple HFS, and Unix Native. You can open an MD1 file with GEAR Software GEAR PRO (Windows) and EZB Systems UltraISO (Windows). To open an MD1 file with GEAR PRO to burn to a CD, follow these steps: Select Open external physical image in the GEAR Project panel Click Open Select Open physical image. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. The second segment of a CD or DVD disc image copied by Alcohol 120% software; part of a sequence of disc image segments (preceded by.MD0, followed by.MD2, etc.); must be joined with the other segments to retrieve the complete disc image. MD1 files are often saved with a.MDF file or a.MDS file, which defines the tracks and data structure used by the disc. Windows Alcohol 120%. Mac MISO-J (to join the segments).",
    "Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows Alcohol 120%. Mac MISO-J (to join the segments).",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".EUI",
    "Ensoniq EPS Compacted Disk Image",
    "3.5",
    "Compressed disk image created by an Ensoniq EPS (Ensoniq Performance Sampler) synthesizer. Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools. FMJ-Software Awave Studio.",
    "Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools. FMJ-Software Awave Studio.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".GCD",
    "Prassi CD Image",
    "3.5",
    "Drawing created with Generic CADD, an older CAD program for Windows; used for creating floor plans, building layouts, and other architectural designs. NOTE: Generic CADD has been replaced by General CADD Pro (GCP), which saves drawings as.GXD files. GCD files can be opened by both Generic CADD and General CADD Pro. Windows General CADD Pro. Generic CADD. Contains exact copy of data from a CD; created by Prassi disc imaging software. Windows Prassi CD Right! Prassi CD Rep Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. Character profile file used by Fallout 2, a post-apocalyptic, role-playing video game; contains profile information for the 3 starting characters that can be chosen in the character creation stage; includes character stats and skills, such as strength, perception, hit points, melee damage, bonus to strength, first aid, and speech. The 3 starting characters in the game are pre-made and each character has a corresponding GCD,.BIO, and.FRM file. These files are all located in the /​premade/​ folder. Windows Bethesda Softworks Fallout 2.",
    "Windows General CADD Pro. Generic CADD. Windows Prassi CD Right! Prassi CD Rep Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows Bethesda Softworks Fallout 2.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".BWI",
    "BlindWrite CD/DVD Disc Image",
    "3.5",
    "Disc image originally created by BlindWrite and now also supported by other disc imaging applications; stores a copy of a CD or DVD disc; design for creating game disc backups. You may come across a BWI file if a BlindWrite user attempts to share a backup of a game, CD, or DVD. If you need to open it there are a variety of programs for Windows that support the format, including Alcohol 120% and Daemon Tools. You can also convert the BWI format to a more common format, such as.ISO, with UltraISO. This is especially helpful if the BWI file needs to be opened by macOS user (there are no macOS applications that support the format). BWI files were replaced with.B5I files when version 5 of BlindWrite was released. B5I files were then replaced by.B6I files with the release of BlindWrite 6. Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. H+H Software Virtual CD. DT Soft DAEMON Tools. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. H+H Software Virtual CD. DT Soft DAEMON Tools. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MFI",
    "HDClone MFI Disk Image",
    "3.5",
    "An MFI file is a disk image created by HDClone, a utility for copying and backing up disk images. It is saved in the HDClone MFI image format and stores the entire data from a disk, which includes the file structure and all files and folders from the disk. HDClone was initially released in 2003 to provide various disk image cloning and backing up capabilities. The application supports the backing up of disk images and other mass storage media, the copying of software and operating system installations, and rescuing defective hard disks. Miray Software introduced the HDClone MFI image format when it released HDClone 8 in 2018. HDClone MFI then became the default image format for HDClone 9 in 2019. To create an MFI file with HDClone, select Images, then click FullImage under the Create option. [YYYYMMDD]Image.mfi - The naming convention for MFI files created by HDClone. The [YYYYMMDD] represents the year, month, and day the MFI file was created. For example, an MFI file created on December 15, 2020 would be named 20201215Image.mfi. You can open MFI files with Miray HDClone 8 and later. Version 7 or earlier of HDClone does not support the MFI format. To open an MFI file with HDClone select Images, then click Restore under the Recover option. HDClone is available as a. Edition with limited copying and restoring capabilities. The commercial version of Miray HDClone requires purchase but offers more advanced capabilities. Windows Miray HDClone. Edition. Miray HDClone.",
    "Windows Miray HDClone. Edition. Miray HDClone.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SQFS",
    "Squash FS Bootable File",
    "3.5",
    "Bootable disk image for Unix that can mounted like a physical disk; formatted using the Squash FS compressed read-only file system; often used to install operating system files. The Linux LiveCD installer uses a bootable disk image named livecd.sqfs.. Linux Linux installation with Squash FS support.",
    "Linux Linux installation with Squash FS support.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".FLP",
    "Floppy Disk Image",
    "3.5",
    "An FLP file is a project created by FL Studio, an application used to compose arrange, record, and edit music. It contains all of the data associated with a project, including demo plugins and music track settings. FLP files do not store the actual audio that appears in the project. Instead, the files contain references to the audio files. Therefore, if you move an FLP file or the files it references, such as a.WAV sample file, or SimSynth or DrumSynth preset file, the project may not play correctly in FL Studio. NOTE: FL Studio was formerly known as FruityLoops, which was originally released in 1998. FruityLoops became FL Studio with the release of version 4 in March 2003. Mac LMMS. Windows Image-Line FL Studio. LMMS. Linux LMMSFree. XML-based file that references all files used by a specific Macromedia Flash Project. Mac Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial Windows Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial. Flipchart presentation created with Promethean educational lesson-planning programs such as ActivPrimary and ActivStudio; contains an interactive lesson plan that can be interacted with using electronic whiteboard hardware; used by teachers to store lesson plans for students. Prometheans software product ActivInspire uses the.FLIPCHART extension, but can still open FLP files. NOTE: If FruityLoops or FL Studio are installed on the same computer as ActivPrimary or ActivStudio, the Flipchart file association may be incorrectly assigned to FruityLoops or FL Studio. You can open the Flipchart files directly in Promethean software by selecting File → Open... Mac Promethean Activprimary —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivStudio —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivInspire. Windows Promethean Activprimary —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivStudio —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivInspire. Linux Promethean Activprimary —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivStudio —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivInspire. An FLP file is a disk image created from a 3.5 floppy disk. Users often create FLP files using an MS-DOS program or a program from an older version of Windows. The files can be used for storing bootable files and booting up guest operating systems on virtualized platforms. They may also be used for copying the contents of a disk to multiple diskettes. The floppy disk image program named fdimage.exe is a common MS-DOS program used to created FLP files. It is also included with FreeBSD installation CDs. You can open and extract files from an FLP file using MagicISO (Windows). You can also add your FLP file as a floppy drive in VMWare Fusion (Mac), VMWare Workstation (Windows, Linux), and VMWare Workstation Player (Windows, Linux). Mac VMware Fusion 13. Windows VMware Workstation 16 Pro. VMware Workstation Player. Microsoft Virtual Server. Towodo Floppy Image MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial DiskWrite. Linux VMware Workstation 16 Pro. VMware Workstation PlayerFree. An FLP file contains a project created by Poklik FlipAnim, an online program used to create flipbook animations. It stores one or more drawings that make up a flipbook animation. FLP files also contain the settings for the animation, such as page speed, page transparency, and the zoom. FLP file open in Poklik FlipAnim You can save a FlipAnim project as an FLP file by clicking the gear icon, then selecting the document icon with the red download arrow. Your web browser will then download the FLP file to your computer. You can open a FlipAnim project by clicking the gear icon, selecting the document icon with the green upload arrow, then choosing the FLP file you want to upload. Your browser will then upload the FLP file and the FlipAnim web program will open the project. NOTE: FLP files were replaced by.FLIPANIM files by FlipAnim to save projects. flipanim_project_<YYYYMMDD>_<TTTT>.flp - The default filename given to FlipAnim projects downloaded as FLP files. The YYYYMMDD stands for the year, month, and day the file was downloaded and the TTTT stands for the time it was downloaded. For example, an FLP file downloaded on April 16, 2019 at 10:59 am is named flipanim_project_20190416_1059.flp. Web Poklik FlipAnimFree.",
    "Mac LMMS. Windows Image-Line FL Studio. LMMS. Linux LMMS. Mac Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial. Windows Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial. Mac Promethean Activprimary —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivStudio —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivInspire. Windows Promethean Activprimary —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivStudio —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivInspire. Linux Promethean Activprimary —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivStudio —  (Discontinued). Trial Promethean ActivInspire. Mac VMware Fusion 13. Windows VMware Workstation 16 Pro. VMware Workstation Player. Microsoft Virtual Server. Towodo Floppy Image MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial DiskWrite. Linux VMware Workstation 16 Pro. VMware Workstation Player. Web Poklik FlipAnim.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".DVDR",
    "DVD/CD-R Master Image",
    "3.5",
    "Disc image file used by DiskImageMounter, a utility included with Mac OS X; stores the contents of a DVD or rewritable CD; may also use the.CDR file extension; enables virtual discs to be mounted without the physical disc. Mac Apple DiskImageMounter.",
    "Mac Apple DiskImageMounter.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".HFS",
    "HFS Disk Image File",
    "3.5",
    "Disk image file created in the Hierarchical System Format (HFS), the file system format used by Mac computers; contains files and folders as they would be stored by a Mac computer; can be opened by 7-Zip on PCs. Windows File Viewer Plus. 7-ZipFree.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. 7-Zip.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SWM",
    "Split Windows Imaging Format File",
    "3.5",
    "File created by ImageX, a command-line program that allows users to create Windows disk images; contains one of multiple part files that have been split from a.WIM file; commonly used to create multiple smaller CD image files from a larger WIM file located on a DVD. SWM files are typically located within a sources directory on each media disc. They may be reconstructed into the original WIM file using XImage. Windows Microsoft ImageX Included with OS. ExtractNowFree.",
    "Windows Microsoft ImageX Included with OS. ExtractNow.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".P01",
    "Toast CD Image",
    "3.5",
    "Copy of a CD saved as a disk image by Toast, a disc creation program for the Macintosh. Windows EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster. PowerISO. Mac Roxio Toast 20. CD or DVD disc image created by GEAR disc burning software. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. Recovery volume used by QuickPar, a parchive (parity archive) volume creating utility; contains a parity volume in PAR1 format; may be referenced to recover a file archive when one of the files in the archive is lost; also referenced to perform data integrity checks on files. To open a P19 file in QuickPar, click Open, navigate to your file, and click Open. Parchive recovery volumes are named with ascending number extensions, e.g., P01, P02, P03, P04, etc. NOTE: The.PAR and.PAR2 extensions are used for index files created with Parchive version 1 and 2 respectively. Windows QuickParFree.",
    "Windows EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster. PowerISO. Mac Roxio Toast 20. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows QuickPar.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VCD",
    "FarStone Virtual Drive",
    "3.5",
    "A VCD file is a disc image of a CD or DVD and is recognized by Windows as an actual disc, even though the data is on the hard drive. VCD files allow CDs and DVDs to be used without requiring physical drives and is useful for mounting discs on network computers. If you receive a VCD file, you can open it with select CD and DVD burning and ripping programs, such as H+H Software Virtual CD, Roxio Popcorn, Roxio Toast, and Roxio Creator NXT Pro. VCD files can also be played with the free MPlayer. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. WinMount International WinMount. Mac Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. MPlayerFree. Copy of a CD or unprotected DVD saved as a compressed image, or virtual disc; can be run in place of the original disc in any one of up to 23 virtual drives. Windows FarStone VirtualDrive 16 Pro. EZB Systems UltraISO. File used by the Valve Source game engine; created by the Faceposer tool, which is included with the Source SDK; saves facial expressions, lip-syncs, and motion sequences for actors in the scene; often used for scripting cutscenes. VCD files are saved in an XML-like text format. Windows Valve Source SDKFree. Data dump generated by logic simulation programs and saved in a text format; contains header information, variable definitions, and value changes for specified variables; value changes are given in scalar or vector format, based on the type of variable. Windows Altera Quartus II —  (Discontinued). Trial Linux GTKWave.",
    "Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. WinMount International WinMount. Mac Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. MPlayer. Windows FarStone VirtualDrive 16 Pro. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows Valve Source SDK. Windows Altera Quartus II —  (Discontinued). Trial. Linux GTKWave.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".OMG",
    "Image File",
    "3.4",
    "Compressed audio file that uses Sonys OpenMG technology; based on Sonys ATRAC3 format, but also includes digital rights management DRM to prevent files from being copied illegally; supported by SonicStage, Sonys music jukebox program. Windows Sony SonicStage —  (Discontinued). Disk image created by O&O DiskImage, a Disk image creation and file backup application; contains a perfect copy of the original disk image; the disk image copy includes all the sectors of the drive and creates an image of the file system with all the files and folders; similar to.ISO,.BIN, and.DMG disk image files. To create the OMG file: Select the Start drive imaging option on the main Start page or select Start → Drive image from the menu. Check the box next to the source drive youd like to create the image from and select Start drive imaging. A Drive Imaging window will open with the Target directory highlighted and other imaging options. Once you tailor the options to your liking, click Start. When the process is completed, click Close. To restore an image using the OMG file: Select the Start restoration option on the main Start page or select Start → Drive restoration from the menu. Navigate to the OMG file youd like to restore and click Open. Check the box next to the source drive youd like to restore the drive from under Source drive, then check the box next to the drive you want to restore to under Target drive, and select Start drive restoration. A Drive restoration window will open with the Target directory highlighted and other restoration options. Once you tailor the options to your liking, click Start. When the process is completed, click Close. To convert the OMG file to a.VHD file: Select Tools → Convert image file.. from the menu. Select Image files (OMG) under Source, click Add, navigate to your OMG file, select Open, check the box next to your contained volume, and then click OK. Select Virtual hard disk (VHD) under Target, customize any options you want (note the Target path where your VHD file will be created), and click Start. Once the process is complete, click Close. NOTE: You can convert a VHD file to OMG by reversing the Source and Target file selections. Image20131215.omg - Name of an OMG file that was created on December 15th, 2013. O&O DIskImage creates files by listing the type (Image) and then the date it was created. Conversion20131217.omg - Name of an OMG file that was created on December 17th, 2013. O&O DIskImage creates files by listing the type (Conversion) and then the date it was created. Windows O&O Software O&O DiskImage 8.",
    "Windows Sony SonicStage —  (Discontinued). Windows O&O Software O&O DiskImage 8.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".TC",
    "TrueCrypt Volume",
    "3.4",
    "Virtual encrypted disk created by TrueCrypt, an open-source disk encryption program that creates real-time (on-the-fly) encrypted volumes. Windows TrueCrypt —  (Discontinued). Data file saved by Tellico, a Linux KDE program used to manage collections of information; may contain data such as titles, authors, cover images, genres, and publication years; can be used for tracking information about books, videos, music, video games, coins, trading cards, and wines. Linux TellicoFree.",
    "Windows TrueCrypt —  (Discontinued). Linux Tellico.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".HC",
    "VeraCrypt Container",
    "3.4",
    "An HC file is a Holy C source code file, which is used with the TempleOS operating system. It contains code that is meant to be compiled within TempleOS. HC files can be opened and edited in any text editor, but can be compiled and run only in TempleOS. In the early 2000s, programmer Terry A. Davis began creating an operating system that came to be known as TempleOS. Davis, who was a schizophrenic, claimed that God commanded him to create TempleOS. While the operating system is most useful for creating simple video games, it includes a number of biblical references, including a game named After Egypt. Davis claimed that a number of the OSs specifications were set down by God, and after publishing TempleOS in 2013, Davis announced that Gods temple is finished. As part of developing TempleOS, Davis also developed the Holy C programming language. Holy C is a variant of the C programming language that is used only by TempleOS. Those who create TempleOS programs write those programs in Holy C, and they save and distribute their work in HC files. While you can open and edit a Holy C source code file in any text editor, HC files are meant to be edited, compiled, and run within the TempleOS operating system. TempleOS includes its own source code editor that allows developers to open, edit, and create HC files. Windows TempleOS. Any text editor Mac TempleOS. Any text editor Linux TempleOS. Any text editor. An HC file is an encrypted virtual disk created by IDRIX VeraCrypt, a volume creation and encryption program. It contains one or more encrypted files that can be opened only after mounting the HC file in VeraCrypt. To mount an HC file, you must know the password used to create it. HC file open in IDRIX VeraCrypt VeraCrypt is a free, open-source program that allows you to create and encrypt storage volumes. You can use VeraCrypt to encrypt existing physical volumes, such as hard drives or USB flash drives, or create and encrypt new virtual volumes. Virtual volumes created using VeraCrypt are saved as HC files. (Users manually add the.hc extension to their volumes filename while creating it.) When you create an HC file, you specify the volumes size (in KB, MB, or GB) and the password used to protect it. You can also adjust other options, such as the encryption and hash algorithms used to encrypt the volume. To create a new HC file, open VeraCrypt, select Create Volume, and use the Volume Creation Wizard to create an encrypted file container. NOTE: VeraCrypt is the successor to TrueCrypt, a previous encryption tool for desktop PCs. You can open an HC file with IDRIX VeraCrypt (multiplatform). To do so: Open VeraCrypt and select a drive. In the Volume section, select Select File.. Navigate to and open your HC file. Click Mount. Enter the password used to encrypt your HC file and then click OK. VeraCrypt will then mount your HC file in the drive you selected. To access the files your HC file contains, open the drive on which you mounted it. Windows VeraCrypt. Mac VeraCrypt. Linux VeraCryptFree.",
    "Windows TempleOS. Any text editor. Mac TempleOS. Any text editor. Linux TempleOS. Any text editor. Windows VeraCrypt. Mac VeraCrypt. Linux VeraCrypt.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".000",
    "Virtual CD Disc Image File",
    "3.4",
    "An 000 file is a data file created by Indexing Service, a software component of the Windows operating system that creates a searchable file system index from files on the hard drive. The data stored in a 000 file helps Windows perform quick searches. 000 files are saved with several other types of files that make up the search index. Other types may include.BK1,.BK2,.FID,.HSH,.PS1, and.PS2 files. The Indexing Service search index is typically created in the following directory: C:/​System Volume Information/​catalog.wci The index may also be generated for external volumes connected to a Windows computer, such as USB flash drives. If you have a slow CPU, you should consider disabling indexing or selectively disabling indexing on your computer. To do this, click Start or the search bar in Windows and type indexing in the search field. Then, click Indexing Options and remove locations in Windows that you no longer want to be indexed. This will increase the amount of time it takes Windows to search these locations but will also improve the performance of the CPU. Windows Microsoft Windows. Index of cabinet (.CAB) files included in an installation package for a Windows CE device; referenced by the programs installer script and defines the files to be installed. Cabinet index files also include the application name and publisher, the designated directory path for installation, WinCE software requirements, the cabinet architecture, and other information. Linux cabextractFree. Disc image data file created by Virtual CD, a disc imaging program; created by versions 4 and later of the software; contains the data for the disc and is saved alongside a.VC4 file; may also be saved with a.VCX file, which is the CD Editor project that was used to create the 000 and VC4 files. 000 files can be burned to a disc using Virtual CD. However, you need to open the corresponding VC4 file (or VCX file if it exists), which then allows the Virtual CD software to access the disc image. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. File archive compressed by Microsoft DoubleSpace, an older file compression utility included with MS-DOS; can be decompressed and opened on the fly using MS-DOS with the DBLSPACE.BIN compression subsystem installed. Windows Microsoft MS-DOS.",
    "Windows Microsoft Windows. Linux cabextract. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows Microsoft MS-DOS.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".B6I",
    "BlindWrite 6 Disc Image",
    "3.4",
    "Disc image created with BlindWrite, a disc authoring program designed for backing up computer game discs; contains an exact copy of the data from the original disc and can be used to burn a copy to another disc. B6I files may be saved along with a corresponding.B6T file. The B6I file contains the actual data to be copied and the B6T file contains information about the file structure of the disc. The B6T file should be opened in order to mount the disc image. NOTE: BlindWrite 5 saved disc images as.B5I files, while previous versions of BlindWrite used the.BWI file extension. Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MLC",
    "MobaLiveCD File",
    "3.3",
    "Language interface pack (LIP) file used by the Windows operating system; contains translation information that allows a user interface to be presented in a particular language; used for localizing Windows operating system installations. Each LIP requires the installation of a base language, which is the source language used to create the translation for the user interface. LIPs often translate only a subset of user interface elements. NOTE: LIPs must be used by the operating system they support. For example, Windows Vista LIPs cannot be used on Windows 7. Windows Microsoft Windows. File created by MobaLiveCD, a program used for running LiveCDs in Windows (a LiveCD is a CD-ROM disc image that contains a bootable, self-contained operating system); stores LiveCD information after it is created from an.ISO file; used for opening the LiveCD. MobaLiveCD is often used for running Linux LiveCDs within the Windows environment. Windows Mobatek MobaLiveCD.",
    "Windows Microsoft Windows. Windows Mobatek MobaLiveCD.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".B5I",
    "BlindWrite 5 Disk Image",
    "3.3",
    "Disc image file originally created by BlindWrite but now supported by other disc imaging applications; often contains data copied from an audio or data CD; can be burned back to a disc or mounted with disc mounting software. Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".GBI",
    "gBurner Project File",
    "3.3",
    "Disk authoring project created with gBurner, a CD/DVD burning tool; stores a list of files and disc burning settings; can be used to create data and audio CDs, DVDs, bootable disks, and multisession CDs and DVDs. gBurner supports CD-R, CD-RW, DVD-R, DVD+R, DVD-RW, DVD+RW, DVD-R DL, DVD+R DL, and DVD-RAM discs. Windows Power Software gBurner.",
    "Windows Power Software gBurner.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".TAP",
    "Commodore 64 Cassette Tape Image",
    "3.3",
    "Disk image created from a data storage tape formatted for the Commodore 64 (C64) or ZX Spectrum systems; contains an exact copy of the data from the tape in a single file; typically used for games or other types of applications. TAP files may be opened on Windows computers using a C64 or ZX Spectrum emulator. Windows Fuse. CCS C64 Forever Spectrum 128K UnrealSpeccy Zephyr UKV Mac Fuse. Power64 VICE. Linux Fuse. Web C64 online emulatorFree. Tap file used by Tap Tap Revenge, a music game similar to Guitar Hero for the iPhone and iPod Touch; contains a timeline of taps that are displayed when a song is played in Tap Tap Revenge. TAP files can be created and edited using third-party software, but they can only be used by Tap Tap Revenge on the iPhone or iPod Touch. Windows Tap Maker TapCreator Transfer to an Apple iPhone or iPod Touch Mac Tap Maker Transfer to an Apple iPhone or iPod Touch.",
    "Windows Fuse. CCS C64 Forever Spectrum 128K UnrealSpeccy Zephyr UKV. Mac Fuse. Power64 VICE. Linux Fuse. Web C64 online emulator. Windows Tap Maker TapCreator Transfer to an Apple iPhone or iPod Touch. Mac Tap Maker Transfer to an Apple iPhone or iPod Touch.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".UTM",
    "UTM Virtual Machine",
    "3.3",
    "A UTM file is a virtual machine created with UTM emulation software. It contains the contents of a hard drive and memory that are used to load a non-iOS operating system on an iOS device (such as an iPhone or iPad). UTM files may contain Android, Linux, Windows, macOS, or other virtual machines. Virtual machine emulation software allows users to run non-standard operating systems on PCs and other devices. For example, the popular Parallels Desktop application allows Mac users to load and run Windows (and thus, Windows-only applications) on their Macs. UTM is a virtual machine emulator available for iOS devices. It allows iOS users to run Android, Linux, Windows, and other non-iOS operating systems on their iOS devices. In this way, iPhone users can, for example, install the Android operating system and Android-only apps on their iOS devices. UTM files contain a prebuilt virtual machine that can be opened within UTM. Several sample UTM files, which can be used to install and access Android and Linux operating systems, are available on UTMs website. NOTE: When you download a UTM file from UTMs website, you will receive it in compressed.ZIP form. You must decompress the file to use it as a UTM virtual machine. You can open a UTM file (and install the virtual machine it contains) on an iOS device using UTM. To do so, install UTM and move your UTM file to the apps Documents directory. The next time you open UTM, your UTM file will appear as a selectable virtual machine. iOS Turing Software UTMFree.",
    "iOS Turing Software UTM.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".LX01",
    "EnCase Logical Evidence File",
    "3.3",
    "Logical evidence file created by EnCase forensics software; contains multiple files copied from an EnCase evidence (.EX01) file, preview, or smartphone acquisitions; used to store noteworthy evidence for forensic reasons. The LX01 file allows users to group smaller collections of digital evidence without having to load the entire EX01 evidence file to view them. The LX01 format replaced the.L01 format, which provides more advanced security features such as AES256 encryption with keypairs or passwords, LZ compression, and the option for SHA-1 or MD5 hashing. NOTE: OpenText acquired Guidance Software in 2017. Windows OpenText EnCase Forensic. OpenText Encase Forensic Imager. GetData Mount Image Pro.",
    "Windows OpenText EnCase Forensic. OpenText Encase Forensic Imager. GetData Mount Image Pro.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".BDF",
    "MIDAS BDF Disk Image File",
    "3.3",
    "Binary data format readable by forensic data analysis programs; used for identifying raw data recovered from blocks of unallocated space on a hard drive. BDF files are recognized by E-mail Detective, a forensic software program that can extract text and images from AOL email files. Windows Hot Pepper Technology E-mail Detective. Patch format used to update video game ROMs; may also be used to produce patches for binary files. Patching is commonly done to video game ROMs for one of two reasons. One reason is that the user wants to play a game in English that wasnt originally created for English users. The other reason is that the user wants to alter gameplay, such as the level order or character dialogue. The bsdiff/bspatch tools can be used to create and apply BDF patch files: To create a BDF patch file that makes your desired goodfile.123 from your original badfile.123, type bsdiff badfile.123 goodfile.123 patch.bdf. To apply a BDF patch file to your badfile.123, which subsequently creates your goodfile.123, type bspatch badfile.123 goodfile.123 patch.bdf. NOTE: The.BSDIFF file extension may also be used instead of the BDF extension but it is less common. Windows bsdiff/bspatch. Mac MultiPatch. bsdiff/bspatch. Linux bsdiff/bspatchFree. ASCII-based font format used on both Macintosh and Windows systems; stores Adobe screen fonts as human-readable data; must be placed in the system Fonts folder in order to be recognized by applications. BDF files are referenced by programs that access system fonts. Windows FontForge. Fontlab FONmaker —  (Discontinued). Mac FontForge. Linux FontForgeFree. Input file used by MSC Nastran, a program that analyzes structures such as aircraft, buildings, and spacecraft; contains bulk data definitions to be input into MSC Nastran to analyze the stress and strain levels of model structures; includes information such as the name of the bulk data, the number of grids, and the node locations of the elements that make up the models; data appears in the FORTRAN programming language, which was originally developed by IBM, and stands for Formula Translating System. BDF files are separated into 5 sections: Nastran Statement File Management Executive Control Case Control Bulk Data NOTE: The BDF file is similar to the.NAS and.DAT files, which are used to input data by MSC Nastran. Windows MSC Nastran. pynastran. Linux MSC Nastran. Disk image format used by ESO-MIDAS ( European Southern Observatory-Munich Image Data Analysis System) software; used primarily for storing astronomy data, such as stellar and surface photometry. Can be read and analyzed on Unix and Open VMS systems using ESO-MIDAS software. Linux ESO-MIDASFree.",
    "Windows Hot Pepper Technology E-mail Detective. Windows bsdiff/bspatch. Mac MultiPatch. bsdiff/bspatch. Linux bsdiff/bspatch. Windows FontForge. Fontlab FONmaker —  (Discontinued). Mac FontForge. Linux FontForge. Windows MSC Nastran. pynastran. Linux MSC Nastran. Linux ESO-MIDAS.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".CISO",
    "Compact ISO File",
    "3.3",
    "CD or DVD disc image image container format designed for efficient storage and distribution of CD/DVD images; contains an exact duplicate of data from the original disc, without unnecessary junk data.. CISO files are commonly used to store Wii games. The CISO file is the same as an.ISO file except it is compact, which means it removes all the junk data used to pad Wii games to make them appear as full discs. The CISO format features password based encryption and digital signing for software distribution. Windows Wii Backup ManagerFree.",
    "Windows Wii Backup Manager.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".TOC",
    "Brasero Table of Contents File",
    "3.3",
    "A TOC file is an audio file saved in a format compatible with the PlayStation Portable (PSP). It likely contains a song that has been converted to the TOC format for use with Grand Theft Auto: Liberty City Stories or Grand Theft Auto: Vice City Stories. Using the app Exact Audio Copy (EAC), players can import TOC files into these games. Like other audio files created for use on the PSP, TOC files are saved using Adaptive Transform Acoustic Coding (ATRAC) compression. In this way, they are similar to.AA3 and.ATRAC files. To create TOC files, gamers use EAC or a related program, named Rockstar Custom Tracks, to convert.MP3 files to TOC files. They then save their TOC files to a custom tracks sub-directory found within a games SAVEDATA directory. (To do this, a gamer must connect their PSPs removable storage to their computer.). To use a TOC file with Grand Theft Auto: Liberty City Stories or Grand Theft Auto: Vice City Stories: Place the file in either the ULES00182CUSTOMTRACKS (GTA: LCS) or ULES00503CUSTOMTRACKS (GTA: VCS) directory on your PSPs removable storage (found within the PSP/​SAVEDATA directory). Replace your PSPs removable storage. Play the game whose data you modified. In the games Pause menu, select Audio → My songs ON. You can then listen to the audio your TOC file contains on the in-game radio, while driving vehicles. Windows Exact Audio Copy —  (Discontinued). Contains a table of contents for messages stored within a certain Eudora mail folder; lists all the messages that are displayed when a mail folder is opened within Eudora. NOTE: Eudora is no longer actively developed. in.toc - Inbox table of contents out.toc - Outbox table of contents junk.toc - Junkmail table of contents trash.toc - Trash table of contents. Mac QUALCOMM Eudora. Windows QUALCOMM EudoraFree. Data file created by Brasero, a disc authoring program for Unix; defines the table of contents (or structure) of the data stored within a disc image; saved along with a corresponding.BIN file, which contains the actual data. NOTE: Both the TOC and BIN files are required to burn a disc image to a CD or DVD with Brasero. Linux BraseroFree. File used by World of Warcraft, a massively multiplayer online role-playing game (MMORPG); contains table of contents information for a user interface add-on; used to describe the add-on name, description, saved variables, and instructions for how the add-on should be loaded. TOC files contain lines of text with ## tags followed by a series filenames. First, tags describe information for the client. For example, ## Version: 1.2 may provide the add-on version. Second, a list of filenames describes the order in which the add-on files are loaded. NOTE: TOC files often reference Blizzard Texture files (.LUA) and.XML configuration files. Mac Blizzard World of Warcraft. Blizzard World of Warcraft Interface AddOn Kit. Windows Blizzard World of Warcraft. Blizzard World of Warcraft Interface AddOn Kit. Addon Studio for World of Warcraft.",
    "Windows Exact Audio Copy —  (Discontinued). Mac QUALCOMM Eudora. Windows QUALCOMM Eudora. Linux Brasero. Mac Blizzard World of Warcraft. Blizzard World of Warcraft Interface AddOn Kit. Windows Blizzard World of Warcraft. Blizzard World of Warcraft Interface AddOn Kit. Addon Studio for World of Warcraft.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".X64",
    "Commodore 64 Disk Image",
    "3.3",
    "An X64 file contains a disk image dumped from a Commodore 64 hard disk or floppy disk. It stores information about a Commodore 64 game or program in X64 format, which includes a 64-byte header followed by disk image data. X64 files are supported by Various emulators of Commodore 64 (C64), a popular personal computer from the 1980s. X64 file open in VICE 3 The X64 format supports disk images dumped from various types of Commodore disks, including 1541, 1571, and 1581. Teemu Rantanen initially developed it for the Unix-based X64 emulator, which the VICE emulator eventually superseded. You will likely only encounter an X64 file if you are a computing enthusiast attempting to emulate a game or program from the discontinued Commodore 64 computer. Some computing enthusiasts dump disk image data from an actual floppy disk or cartridge and save it in the X64 format to run in an emulator on their computer. However, most enthusiasts download X64 files from websites. NOTE: The X64 format is one of several formats utilized to store Commodore 64 disk images. Some others include.G41 and.D64. You can open X64 files with various emulators, including Vice (multiplatform), WinVICE (Windows), and CCS64 (Windows). You can also upload X64 files to C64 online emulator and run them in a web browser. Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Mac VICE. Web C64 online emulatorFree.",
    "Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Mac VICE. Web C64 online emulator.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PVM",
    "Parallels Virtual Machine",
    "3.3",
    "A PVM file is a virtual machine created by Parallels Desktop for Mac, a virtualization application for Macs that can run other operating systems. It is a macOS package that stores all of the information for a virtual machine required by Parallels Desktop for Mac to load and run an operating system, such as Windows or macOS. PVM file open in Parallels Desktop for Mac 18 Parallels Desktop for Mac is a program that allows Mac users to run Windows, Linux, Android, and additional versions of macOS on their computers. When you create a virtual machine (VM) with Parallels Desktop, the software creates a PVM file to store its contents. To create a PVM file, select File → New.. or click + in the Control Center. Each PVM file is a macOS package that contains all the files Parallels Desktop needs to launch an operating system. One of these files is an.HDD file, which stores the files saved to your virtual machines hard drive. For example, a Windows virtual machines HDD file includes all the Windows program files you have added to that virtual machine. Another file located within a PVM file is the Parallels Desktop Configuration.PVS file, which contains settings for the VM. NOTE: Since PVM files contain all of the information for a VM, you can move a VM to another computer by moving the PMV file. To open a PVM file as a virtual machine, you must install Parallels Desktop for Mac on your Mac. Place your PVM file in the folder you set as the default location for storing virtual machines (Parallels default location is Macintosh HD/​Users/​[username]/​Documents/​Parallels/​). Then, open Parallels, select File → Open, and select your PVM file. Opening a PVM file as a macOS package PVM file open in Apple Finder In macOS, you can right-click a PVM file and select Show Package Contents to view the packages directories and files with Apple Finder. Files you may find stored within a PVM file include PVS, HDD,.LOG,.ISO, and.DAT files. NOTE: Modifying the contents in a PVM file may corrupt the virtual machine. Mac Parallels Desktop for Mac 18. VMware Fusion 13. Index file used for organizing collections of digital content; developed by the Optical Storage Technology Association (OSTA) as part of the Music/Photo/Video (MPV) standard; formatted in XML. PVM files may be generated by various digital media devices such as printers and digital cameras. They can be read by any program that supports PVM format. Mac HP Image Zone Windows OSTA MPV Reader, Writer, or Player HP Image Zone Visual MIBrowser. Digital photo album created with HP digital camera software; contains a list of images imported from an HP digital camera; often named album.pvm or index.pvm; used by HP Image Zone Express, which has been replaced by Photosmart software. Windows HP Image Zone Express.",
    "Mac Parallels Desktop for Mac 18. VMware Fusion 13. Mac HP Image Zone. Windows OSTA MPV Reader, Writer, or Player HP Image Zone Visual MIBrowser. Windows HP Image Zone Express.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".UDF",
    "Universal Disk Format File",
    "3.3",
    "File created in the Universal Disk Format (UDF), a disc imaging format used for storing files on optical media; stores a collection of files using the directory structure specified in the UDF standard; can be used for burning DVDs, CDs, and other optical media; allows files to be deleted and modified on the target disc even after they have been written. The UDF standard is developed and maintained by the Optical Storage Technology Association (OSTA). NOTE: Even though the UDF format is commonly used, the .udf extension is not. Instead, disc burning applications typically save images in their own formats and then burn them using the UDF standard. Windows Nero 2022. MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial 7-Zip. Mac Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. User-defined script written or recorded in Visual Basic that executes a function in Microsoft Excel; can be created by selecting Module from the Insert menu in Excel. Windows No longer supported by Microsoft Excel Mac No longer supported by Microsoft Excel.",
    "Windows Nero 2022. MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial 7-Zip. Mac Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. Windows No longer supported by Microsoft Excel. Mac No longer supported by Microsoft Excel.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".EDK",
    "Ensoniq KT Disk Image",
    "3.3",
    "Copy of a disk formatted for an Ensoniq KT synthesizer. Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".RCL",
    "Easy CD and DVD Creator 6 Project",
    "3.3",
    "File created by Recolored, a program used for colorizing black and white photos; saves an image and the edits applied for adding color to different regions of the image. NOTE: The Recolored software has been discontinued. Windows Recolored. CD or DVD authoring project created with Roxios Easy CD and DVD Creator 6; contains disc structure information and references to files that will be included on the disc. RCL files can also be opened with Creator Classic from Easy Media Creator 7. Windows Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Roxio Easy CD and DVD Creator 6.",
    "Windows Recolored. Windows Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Roxio Easy CD and DVD Creator 6.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".ST",
    "Atari ST Disk Image",
    "3.3",
    "Disk image of an Atari ST program or video game; contains exact data copied from an Atari floppy disk or cartridge; may be opened on a PC using Atari emulation software. The Atari ST was a personal computer released by Atari in 1985. Windows File Viewer Plus. Hatari. Steem ARAnyM Mac Hatari. Nostalgia. ARAnyM Linux Hatari. XSteem STonX ARAnyM. An ST file contains source code written in the Smalltalk programming language. For example, it may contain a Smalltalk expression or script. Developers create ST files while writing Smalltalk programs. ST file open in Microsoft Visual Studio Code Smalltalk is an object-oriented programming language that has influenced many other object-oriented programming languages. It has many variants, including Smalltalk-80, GNU Smalltalk, OpenSmalltalk, Squeak, and Dolphin Smalltalk. Developers creating Smalltalk programs save the source code theyve written as ST files. At runtime, the Smalltalk compiler compiles the code ST files contain to bytecode, which is typically interpreted by a Smalltalk virtual machine. You can open an ST file with Microsoft Visual Studio Code (multiplatform) or any other text editor. Doing so will allow you to view and/or edit the Smalltalk source code your ST file contains. Windows Squeak. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Mac Squeak. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Linux Squeak. GNU Smalltalk. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. Hatari. Steem ARAnyM. Mac Hatari. Nostalgia. ARAnyM. Linux Hatari. XSteem STonX ARAnyM. Windows Squeak. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor. Mac Squeak. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor. Linux Squeak. GNU Smalltalk. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor. Chrome OS Google Chrome Text Included with OS.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".FDD",
    "Parallels Desktop Floppy Disk Image File",
    "3.2",
    "File used by Parallels Desktop, a program used to run Windows on Mac computers; contains a floppy disk image that enables users to load virtual floppy disks without an actual floppy disk drive. FDD files are loaded by the Parallels Desktop virtual machine (VM), and are treated the same as physical floppy disks. Mac Parallels Desktop for Mac 18. Document created by FormDocs, a program used for creating structured forms; may be a multi-page document and stores the layout of labels, text fields, checkboxes, and other form elements; used for filling, printing, and managing digital business forms. Windows FormDocs.",
    "Mac Parallels Desktop for Mac 18. Windows FormDocs.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".T64",
    "Commodore 64 Tape Image File",
    "3.2",
    "A T64 file contains a game or program copied from a Commodore 64 tape storage device. It stores an exact copy of a game or program in the Commodore 64 Tape (T64) format, including a header, a directory of files stored on the tape, and the data for each file. T64 files are supported by select emulators of Commodore 64 (C64), a popular personal computer from the 1980s. T64 file open in VICE 3 Commodore 64 supported various storage devices for transporting and loading data. Examples of supported devices include floppy disks, cartridges, and datasettes (data cassettes that were magnetic tape data storage devices). Miha Peternel developed the T64 format to store game or program data copied from a Commodore tape storage device. The T64 format is one of several formats utilized to store Commodore 64 games or programs that computer enthusiasts load in emulators to run the game or program. Other formats created by Commodore 64 enthusiasts include.G41,.D64, and.X64. You can open T64 files with several emulators, including Vice (multiplatform), WinVICE (Windows), and CCS64 (Windows). You can also upload T64 files to C64 online emulator to run them in a web browser or run T64 files with Joerg Jahnke Mobile C64 on an Android device. Android Joerg Jahnke Mobile C64. Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Mac VICE. Linux VICE. Web C64 online emulatorFree.",
    "Android Joerg Jahnke Mobile C64. Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Mac VICE. Linux VICE. Web C64 online emulator.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".FLG",
    "IsoPuzzle Flag File",
    "3.2",
    "File created by Puppy Linux, an easy-to-use Linux variant; used to mark a certain partition to scan or boot from; referenced during startup and during file system checks; may surface after a bad shutdown of the system; deleted automatically after a successful startup or file system check. FLG files may appear as FSCK.flg or FSCKME.flg, which are similar to FSCK.me files. These files are accessed by the system and are not meant to be opened manually. pupdesk.flg - specifies the startup partition when Linux is started using the PUPMODE=5 parameter. It exists only until the desktop boots up and doesnt get created after that. If a hard/forced shut down occurs, the PUPDESK.flg will be rediscovered during the boot-up and and will force the Xorg Wizard to run instead of starting up the desktop again. Linux Puppy LinuxFree. File used by IsoPuzzle, a program used to recover data from scratched or incompletely burned CD and DVD disc images; contains a list of good sectors in a corresponding.ISO file; marks recoverable sectors so that they can be reconstructed into a new disc image. NOTE: IsoPuzzle does not support Audio CDs, VCDs, or SVCDs. Windows IsoPuzzleFree. File used by the HP system recovery application that is pre-installed on some HP computers; saved in the C:/​System.sav/​Flags/​ directory and contains flags that tell the installer which information to use when running program functions. FLG files are not meant to be opened manually. They are referenced by the HP system recovery software. Windows HP System Recovery Included with OS.",
    "Linux Puppy Linux. Windows IsoPuzzle. Windows HP System Recovery Included with OS.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IMD",
    "ImageDisk Disk Image File",
    "3.2",
    "GIS file that contains georeferencing information for a corresponding raster image, such as a GeoTIFF (.TIF); can store coordinate location information as well as the referenced raster image; maps GIS images onto real-world geography. Windows ERDAS IMAGINE. ESRI ArcGIS Pro. File created by VisioModeler, an older Microsoft program used for creating diagrams; used for storing Object Role Modeling (ORM) information models; used for business data modeling and documentation purposes. Windows Microsoft VisioModeler. An IMD file contains a disk image of a floppy disk, which is also known as diskette. It stores an exact copy of the disk image of the floppy disk and includes information about the disk format. IMD files are typically created by ImageDisk, an MS-DOS based disk imaging utility used to archive and recreate various floppy disk formats. ImageDisk may create IMD files from a variety of floppy disk formats but they must be compatible with PC 765 floppy diskette controller and drives. While IMD files are typically created by ImageDisk, they may also be created and manipulated by pyImageDisk. Windows ImageDisk. pyImageDiskFree.",
    "Windows ERDAS IMAGINE. ESRI ArcGIS Pro. Windows Microsoft VisioModeler. Windows ImageDisk. pyImageDisk.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".DISC",
    "Roxio Toast Document",
    "3.2",
    "A DISC file is a project file created by Roxio Toast, a multimedia disc authoring and burning application. It contains the saved settings for a data, audio, video, or, copy, or conversion disc project. DISC files also include references to the locations of data or multimedia files included in the project and burning settings. DISC file open in Roxio Toast 18 Titanium DISC files are the native format for Toast projects. These project files can be used for burning optical discs as well as disc image files. The native extension used for Toast disc images is.TOAST. To save a project as a DISC file with Toast, select File → Save. To open a project saved in a DISC file, select File → Open. The following are the different types of projects DISC files may store: Data - Burn any file or folder on a disc for archiving, transferring, backing up purposes. Audio - Burn audio to a CD, music DVD or MP3 disc to be played in a computer, home or car stereo, or DVD player. Video - Create VCDs, SVCDs, DVD-Video discs, Blu-ray or HighDef DVDs to be used in a computer or DVD player. Copy - Copy a CD, DVD, BD, or disc image file. Conversion - Convert a video file to another video format. Mac Roxio Toast 20.",
    "Mac Roxio Toast 20.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".AFF",
    "AFF Disk Image",
    "3.2",
    "An AFF file is a spellcheck dictionary file used by Kingsoft WPS Office and Apache OpenOffice, which are free Office suite applications. It contains information that describes the accompanying.DIC spellcheck dictionary file. The AFF and DIC files are used to add new spellchecking dictionaries for different languages, such as Italian and French, to WPS Office. Users typically download.ZIP dictionary packages that contain the AFF and DIC files, extract the contents, then install them in WPS Office. Windows Kingsoft WPS Office. Apache OpenOffice. Mac Kingsoft WPS Office. Apache OpenOffice. Linux Kingsoft WPS Office. Apache OpenOfficeFree. Disk image created in the Advanced Forensics Format (AFF), an open file format used to capture digital evidence for legal proceedings; can store both raw disk data and arbitrary metadata; supports two kinds of compression, zlib and LZMA. An AFF file can be divided into a split archive of multiple files that use the.AFD file extension. Advanced Forensics Format programs can also extract metadata from the disk image and store it separately in an.AFM file. Version 2 of the AFF specification also supports encryption. Windows AFFLIB —  (Discontinued). PassMark OSForensics. GetData Mount Image Pro. Linux AFFLIB —  (Discontinued).",
    "Windows Kingsoft WPS Office. Apache OpenOffice. Mac Kingsoft WPS Office. Apache OpenOffice. Linux Kingsoft WPS Office. Apache OpenOffice. Windows AFFLIB —  (Discontinued). PassMark OSForensics. GetData Mount Image Pro. Linux AFFLIB —  (Discontinued).",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".TZX",
    "ZX Spectrum Tape Image File",
    "3.1",
    "Exact copy of a cassette for the Sinclair ZX Spectrum, a home computer released in the UK in 1982; used primarily for basic word processing, graphics programs, and video games; can be run on a PC using a Spectrum emulation program. Windows Spectaculator RealSpectrum ZX Spin Speccy Klive Zecut0r Z80 emulator GLECK vbSpec M.E.S.S. Mac ZXSP-OSX Fuse M.E.S.S. Linux XZX-Pro Fuse Spectemu FBZX Aspectrum. A TZX file is a data exchange file created by version 4 or later of TimeZero Navigator or TimeZero Professional, which are marine navigation programs. It contains some combination of user-entered data, such as navigational routes, routes, marks, layers, tracks, and objects. TimeZero users use TZX files to share data with other users. Mariners use TimeZero Navigator and Professional to load topographical charts and plot marine navigation routes and points of interest. If a user wants to share the data they entered in TimeZero Navigator or Professional with another TimeZero user, they can export it as a TZX file. Each TZX file contains all the routes, marks, layers, tracks, and other objects a user entered in their current TimeZero session. However, TZX files do not contain the charts upon which they were created. Therefore, before opening a TZX file, you must install the corresponding chart in TimeZero Navigator or Professional, to ensure you can view the files data correctly. NOTE: The TZX format replaced the.TZD format used in earlier versions of TimeZero Navigator and Professional. You can open a TZX file in TimeZero Navigator or TimeZero Professional (Windows). To do so, select Import/Export from TimeZeros menu, and then navigate to and open your TZX file. Windows TimeZero Navigator. TimeZero Professional.",
    "Windows Spectaculator RealSpectrum ZX Spin Speccy Klive Zecut0r Z80 emulator GLECK vbSpec M.E.S.S. Mac ZXSP-OSX Fuse M.E.S.S. Linux XZX-Pro Fuse Spectemu FBZX Aspectrum. Windows TimeZero Navigator. TimeZero Professional.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".I00",
    "DVD Shrink Part 1 File",
    "3.1",
    "The first file of a group of disk image files saved by DVD Shrink; designed to fit a DVD-Video disc on a single DVD-R disc; usually saved as part of a sequence of files, i.e. .I00,.I01,.I02, etc.. Also saved with a corresponding.MDS file that defines the data structure of the disc. Windows DVD Shrink Alcohol 120%. DT Soft DAEMON Tools.",
    "Windows DVD Shrink Alcohol 120%. DT Soft DAEMON Tools.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IMZ",
    "WinImage Compressed Disk Image",
    "3.1",
    "An IMZ file contains a compressed disk image created with WinImage, a disk image utility. It stores an exact copy of a physical disk, but is also compressed to save disk space. IMZ files can be mounted as a physical disk when opened with WinImage. IMZ files are the compressed version of.IMA files. They may be created to store multiple types of disk images, such as a hard drive, CD, or virtual hard drive (VHD). However, they are most often created to store a disk image of a 1.44MB 3.5 floppy disk. Windows WinImage.",
    "Windows WinImage.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SMI",
    "Self-Mounting Disk Image",
    "3.1",
    "An SMI file is a disk image file created by Aladdin ShrinkWrap or Apple Disk Copy in Mac OS Classic (Mac OS 9 or earlier). It contains a disk image that mounts as a disk on the Desktop when opened. SMI files were designed to enable floppy disks and CDs to be available as digital downloads. The files could be double-clicked, which would then load a virtual disk on the Desktop of the computer. When Mac OS X replaced Mac OS Classic, the DiskImageMounter utility was bundled with Mac OS X to mount disk images. Self-mounting images became less popular as SMI files were replaced by.DMG files in Mac OS X. Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Apple Disk Copy Aladdin ShrinkWrap. Audiovisual presentation created using the Synchronized Multimedia Integration Language (SMIL); used for multimedia presentations and slide shows; typically contains references to a sequence of media files as well as text and other data. SMIL files often use the.SMIL extension to avoid confusion with the Mac OS 9 SMI disk image extension. The.smi extension is commonly seen on Windows computers. Android RealNetworks RealTimes Video Maker. Windows ACD Systems ACDSee Photo Studio. RealNetworks RealPlayer 20/20. Mac Apple QuickTime Player Included with OS. iOS RealNetworks RealTimes: Video Maker. An SMI file is a plain text subtitles file written in the SAMI (Synchronized Accessible Media Interchange) format. It contains subtitle text, timing information, and styling information. SMI files can be used to show subtitles in a variety of media players. SMI file open in GitHub Atom SMI files always include <SAMI> as their first line of text. The file then goes on to define its subtitles margin, font, text alignment, and color. The files included subtitles begin at the <BODY> tag. Each line of subtitle text is paired with a <SYNC Start=> tag, which specifies when that line of text appears on-screen. Typically, SYNC Start times are written in milliseconds (so a line of text paired with <SYNC Start=3400> will appear 3400 ms into a video). NOTE: The SAMI format was developed by Microsoft, as an extension of HTML. How do I convert SMI subtitles to SRT subtitles? The.SRT subtitle format is more common than the SMI format. Therefore, you might want to convert your SMI subtitles to the SRT format. SubtitleTools.com has a converter, called Convert Subtitles to Srt, that you can use to convert SMI files to the SRT format. MConverter.eu also allows users to convert SMI files to the SRT format. You can use an SMI file as a subtitles track in the following media players: VideoLAN VLC media player (cross-platform) MPlayer (cross-platform) Microsoft Windows Media Player (Windows) For example, to use an SMI file as a subtitle track in VLC media player: Open VLC media player and begin playing the video you want to use your SMI file with. From the VLC Media Player menu bar, select Subtitles → Add Subtitle File.. Navigate to and open your SMI file. If you need to edit the subtitles an SMI file contains, you can open and edit the file in any text editor, such as Microsoft Notepad (Windows), Apple TextEdit (Mac), or GitHub Atom (cross-platform). Android Bolero33 Soul Movie. J2 Interactive MX Player. Windows Microsoft Windows Media Player Included with OS. VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. EZTitles. URUWorks Subtitle Workshop. Any text editor Mac MPlayer. VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor iOS olimsoft OPlayer.",
    "Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Apple Disk Copy Aladdin ShrinkWrap. Android RealNetworks RealTimes Video Maker. Windows ACD Systems ACDSee Photo Studio. RealNetworks RealPlayer 20/20. Mac Apple QuickTime Player Included with OS. iOS RealNetworks RealTimes: Video Maker. Android Bolero33 Soul Movie. J2 Interactive MX Player. Windows Microsoft Windows Media Player Included with OS. VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. EZTitles. URUWorks Subtitle Workshop. Any text editor. Mac MPlayer. VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor. iOS olimsoft OPlayer.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IMAGE",
    "Apple Disk Image",
    "3.1",
    "File containing exact copy of data from a Mac-formatted disk; can be mounted on the desktop like a CD or other disk; used by Disk Copy and ShrinkWrap. Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. Disk Copy ShrinkWrap. File used by OpenQuaq, a free virtual 3D online collaboration program; saves a base image that is used for running either the client or the server OpenQwaq application. Mac OpenQwaq. Windows OpenQwaq. Linux OpenQwaqFree. An IMAGE file is a virtual image that can be run in a Squeak virtual machine (VM). It contains a saved instance of a developers Squeak development session. IMAGE files can be used to create Squeak VMs on Windows, Mac, and Linux, as well as in web-based Squeak VMs. IMAGE file open in SqueakJS Squeak is a programming language and development environment that was created by developers at Apple and Disney. The Squeak language is based on the Smalltalk-80 language. Squeak is now open-source, meaning anyone can download the Squeak development environment and create programs using the Squeak programming language. The Squeak development environment comes packaged as an.EXE file,.DMG file, or other executable, which is used to launch a Squeak VM. In their VM, developers can develop programs using the Squeak programming language. When a developer saves a Squeak development session, the session is saved as an IMAGE file. A developer can load their IMAGE file the next time they start a Squeak VM, to resume their development session. You can open an IMAGE file in Squeak (cross-platform) or SqueakJS (Web). For example, to open an IMAGE file in SqueakJS, go to https://squeak.js.org/run/ and drag and drop your IMAGE file onto the page. Mac Squeak. Windows Squeak. Linux Squeak. Web SqueakJSFree.",
    "Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. Disk Copy ShrinkWrap. Mac OpenQwaq. Windows OpenQwaq. Linux OpenQwaq. Mac Squeak. Windows Squeak. Linux Squeak. Web SqueakJS.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".CUE",
    "CDRWIN Cue Sheet",
    "3.1",
    "A CUE file is a text file that defines the way tracks are organized on compact discs and super-audio CDs (SACDs). It may contain the song name, performer, and length of each track on the disc. CUE files are written in a standard format and therefore are recognized by most CD playback and editing programs. Windows Nero 2022. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Foobar2000. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. Alcohol 120%. Lightning UK! ImgBurn. VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor Mac Roxio Toast 20. NTI Dragon Burn 4. Burn. VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor Linux VideoLAN VLC media playerFree. Text file that defines how the tracks of CDRWin disc image are organized; usually saved with a corresponding.BIN file that stores the data from the disc. Windows Engelmann Media CDRWIN —  (Discontinued). Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows Nero 2022. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Foobar2000. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. Alcohol 120%. Lightning UK! ImgBurn. VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor. Mac Roxio Toast 20. NTI Dragon Burn 4. Burn. VideoLAN VLC media player. Any text editor. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. Windows Engelmann Media CDRWIN —  (Discontinued). Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".WOZ",
    "Apple II Disk Image",
    "3.0",
    "A WOZ file is a disk image saved in the WOZ binary format, which contains program data in the same structure as an Apple II, or Apple ][, floppy disk. It also stores metadata about the disk image, such as the disk name, publisher, language, and system requirements. WOZ files are typically used for Apple II games, utilities, and educational programs. The WOZ format, which is named after Apple co-founder Steve Wozniak, was released in 2018 and came from the Applesauce project. It is developed by John K. Morris with the purpose of providing a simple disk image format that accurately stores data as it is encoded in an Apple II floppy disk. WOZ files can be opened by emulators, such as MAME and OpenEmulator. Windows MAME. microM8. Mac OpenEmulator. microM8. Linux MAME. microM8Free.",
    "Windows MAME. microM8. Mac OpenEmulator. microM8. Linux MAME. microM8.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".AFD",
    "AFF Disk Image Part File",
    "3.0",
    "CAD file created by Alphacam, a CAD woodworking application; contains a flame drawing, which specifies a 3D design that will be cut using a flame tool; can be used for cutting simple parts or complex five-axis 3D components. Windows Planit Alphacam. Part of a split archive created from an.AFF (Advanced Forensics Format) disk image; allows large AFF files to be split into smaller segments so they can be managed easier; helps with 32-bit file systems where the file size limit is 2GB; also helps with storage limitations with DVD backups. Windows AFFLIB —  (Discontinued). PassMark OSForensics. Linux AFFLIB —  (Discontinued).",
    "Windows Planit Alphacam. Windows AFFLIB —  (Discontinued). PassMark OSForensics. Linux AFFLIB —  (Discontinued).",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VDI",
    "Virtuo CD Manager Disk Image",
    "3.0",
    "A VDI file is a virtual disk image used by Oracle VM VirtualBox, an open-source desktop virtualization program. It is used to create a VirtualBox virtual machine. Virtual machines allow users to run additional operating systems and programs on their PCs. Oracle VM VirtualBox is a free program that allows users to create and manage virtual machines. Many businesses use VirtualBox to allow their employees to run additional operating systems on their PCs. For example, a Mac user can use VirtualBox to run Windows on their Mac. A VDI file is a disk image that a VirtualBox user can use to start a virtual machine. Continuing the example above, a user creating a Windows virtual machine could use a Windows VDI file as that machines hard disk. You can open a VDI file in Oracle VM VirtualBox (cross-platform) as part of creating a new virtual machine. To do so: Place your VDI file in VirtualBoxs HardDisks directory. Open VirtualBox and create a new virtual machine that uses the same operating system as your VDI file. When asked to provide a hard disk image, select Use existing hard disk. Then, select the folder icon. In the Virtual Media Manager window, select Add. Then, select your VDI file. Finish creating your virtual machine. Windows Oracle VM VirtualBox. Aryson VDI Recovery. Mac Oracle VM VirtualBox. Linux Oracle VM VirtualBoxFree. Exact copy, or image, of a CD created with Virtuo CD Manager. Windows EZB Systems UltraISO. WinMount International WinMount. MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial. File used by StepMania, a rhythmic dance program that includes support for dance pads; contains debug information used for reporting program crashes in Windows; stored in a binary format and usually has the filename StepMania.vdi. Windows StepManiaFree.",
    "Windows Oracle VM VirtualBox. Aryson VDI Recovery. Mac Oracle VM VirtualBox. Linux Oracle VM VirtualBox. Windows EZB Systems UltraISO. WinMount International WinMount. MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial. Windows StepMania.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".XA",
    "CD-ROM eXtended Architecture Disc Image",
    "3.0",
    "Audio file used by The Sims, a game where players build and simulate cities; contains music or other sounds for the game; commonly stored within The Sims archive files (.FAR files). XA files can be used to modify The Sims audio with custom sounds. However, these modifications are not supported by Electronic Arts. Windows Electronic Arts The Sims. VideoLAN VLC media player. Mac Electronic Arts The Sims. VideoLAN VLC media player. Macgo Mac Media Player. Linux VideoLAN VLC media playerFree. An XA file is an audio file used by a PlayStation (PS1) video game. It contains dialogue, sound effects, or music played in the game. Many games include XA files. Most gamers will never encounter XA files. However, some dedicated gamers have created utilities that allow them to extract XA files from PS1 games. This allows the gamers to play the files and convert them to more common audio formats, using PlayStation game data utilities. Some games that use XA files include: NBA Live 97 Bust a Groove 2 Mega Man Legends NOTE:.STR files use audio saved in the XA format. You can open an XA file and play the audio it contains with PSMPlay (Windows), PsxMC (Windows), or XA Audio Converter (Windows). PSMPlay, PsxMC, and XA Audio Converter can all convert XA files to the.WAV audio format. Windows File Viewer Plus — Get it from Microsoft. PSMPlay. PsxMC. XA Audio ConverterFree. An XA file is a disc image saved in the eXtended Architecture (XA) format. It contains a copy of a CD-ROM saved in the Mode 2 layout, which includes a 16-byte header and compressed video and audio data organized into tracks that support 2336 bytes of data per sector. Philips, Sony, and Microsoft introduced the XA format in 1988 to improve the CD-ROM standard. The CD-i storage format later replaced XA in the early-1990s. One of the main aspects of XA is that it stores data sectors in a different layout than the Yellow Book CD-ROM standard. An aspect of the different structure is that it makes error correction code storage optional, resulting in the storage of more video data. You can open an XA file with GEAR Software GEAR PRO (Windows) and EZB Systems UltraISO (Windows). To open an XA file with GEAR PRO to burn to a CD, follow these steps: Select Open external physical image in the GEAR Project panel Click Open Select Open physical image. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows Electronic Arts The Sims. VideoLAN VLC media player. Mac Electronic Arts The Sims. VideoLAN VLC media player. Macgo Mac Media Player. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. Windows File Viewer Plus — Get it from Microsoft. PSMPlay. PsxMC. XA Audio Converter. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".BWZ",
    "WinImage Batch Configuration File",
    "3.0",
    "Batch configuration file created with WinImage, a disk image creation utility; contains settings that can be loaded into the WinImage Batch Assistant; used for automating the creation of multiple disk images. To export a BWZ file from WinImage, click the Save.. button in the lower-right corner of the Batch Assistant window. Windows Gilles Vollant WinImage.",
    "Windows Gilles Vollant WinImage.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".GKH",
    "Ensoniq EPS Family Disk Image",
    "3.0",
    "Disk image format used by Ensoniq synthesizers, including the EPS, VFX, SD, TS, and ASR models; stores voice and song data on disks used by the synthesizers. Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PARTIMG",
    "Partimage File",
    "3.0",
    "Disk partition saved to a disk image file with the Unix Partition Image program; may be compressed using.GZIP or.BZIP2 compression and can be split into multiple files that can be copied onto more than one disk. Linux Partition Image.",
    "Linux Partition Image.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IBADR",
    "IsoBuster Address File",
    "3.0",
    "An IBADR file is a data file created by Smart Projects IsoBuster, a program used to recover data from damaged disks and file systems. It contains addresses of data blocks stored in an.IBDAT file. IBADR files are created with IBDAT files by IsoBuster to debug issues encountered by the program. The addresses contained in an IBADR file only reference blocks of data from a problematic large drive that are necessary for debugging the issue with the drive. This allows small disk image files to be created for much larger drives. NOTE: IBADR files are extremely rare since they are only used for debugging purposes. Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PXI",
    "PlexTools Disc Image",
    "3.0",
    "Source code file written in Pyrex, a programming language and compiler used for building Python modules with C-like syntax and performance; used similarly to the #include directive from the C language and is used for textually including code into a.PYX file. PXI files differ from.PXD files, which are definition, or header, files that contain declarations of Pyrex classes and methods. Windows Pyrex. Cython. Mac Pyrex. Cython. Linux Pyrex. CythonFree. Disc image created with PlexTools disc copying software (bundled with Plextor drives); commonly used to make backups of CDs and DVDs. Windows PlexTools Professional EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "Windows Pyrex. Cython. Mac Pyrex. Cython. Linux Pyrex. Cython. Windows PlexTools Professional EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MD1",
    "Alcohol Disk Image Segment 2",
    "3.0",
    "An MD1 file is a CD-ROM Mode 1 (MD1) disc image created by GEAR Software GEAR PRO disc-burning software. It contains a copy of a CD saved in the Mode 1 layout, which includes tracks subdivided into blocks containing header information, and 2048 bytes of data per sector. GEAR PRO users typically save data CD images as MD1 files. GEAR PRO allows you to manage CD (audio and data), DVD, and Blu-ray images, which includes burning and duplicating discs. When extracting a disc image from a CD, you can save it as an MD1 file. The MD1 format supports any CD-ROM Mode 1 compatible CD image, which includes ISO/Joliet, ISO, ISO/UDF, UDF, Apple HFS, and Unix Native. You can open an MD1 file with GEAR Software GEAR PRO (Windows) and EZB Systems UltraISO (Windows). To open an MD1 file with GEAR PRO to burn to a CD, follow these steps: Select Open external physical image in the GEAR Project panel Click Open Select Open physical image. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. The second segment of a CD or DVD disc image copied by Alcohol 120% software; part of a sequence of disc image segments (preceded by.MD0, followed by.MD2, etc.); must be joined with the other segments to retrieve the complete disc image. MD1 files are often saved with a.MDF file or a.MDS file, which defines the tracks and data structure used by the disc. Windows Alcohol 120%. Mac MISO-J (to join the segments).",
    "Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows Alcohol 120%. Mac MISO-J (to join the segments).",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MD2",
    "Alcohol Disk Image Segment 2",
    "3.0",
    "An MD2 file is a 3D model used by id Software Quake 2. Primarily, MD2 files contain models of player characters, non-player characters, but some MD2 files instead contain weapon or item models. Only Quake 2 and its expansions use MD2 files. MD2 file open in chUmbaLum sOft MilkShape 3D Quake 2 is the second entry in the popular Quake video game series. It was released in 1997, with various expansion packs and platform ports released later. The game and its expansions use 3D models saved in the MD2 format. Each MD2 file stores information about a models geometry and the animations used to animate it. It also contains data used to load and apply textures (which are saved in separate files) to the model. You can open an MD2 file with chUmbaLum sOft MilkShape 3D (Windows), QuArK (Windows), or wal (multiplatform). You can use chUmbaLum sOft MilkShape 3D to convert MD2 files to many other 3D model formats. To do so: Import your MD2 file into MilkShape 3D. Select File → Export. Choose the format to which you want to convert your MD2 file. Mac id Software Quake 2. wal. Windows id Software Quake 2. chUmbaLum sOft MilkShape 3D. QuArK. wal. Linux id Software Quake 2. walFree. The third segment of a CD or DVD disc image copied by Alcohol 120% software; part of a sequence of disc image segments (each part ends in.MD0,.MD1, then MD2, etc.); must be joined with the other segments to retrieve the complete disc image. MD2 files are often saved with a.MDF file or a.MDS file, which defines the tracks and data structure used by the disc. Mac MISO-J (to join the segments) Windows Alcohol 120%.",
    "Mac id Software Quake 2. wal. Windows id Software Quake 2. chUmbaLum sOft MilkShape 3D. QuArK. wal. Linux id Software Quake 2. wal. Mac MISO-J (to join the segments). Windows Alcohol 120%.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".P01",
    "GEAR CD/DVD Disc Image",
    "3.0",
    "Copy of a CD saved as a disk image by Toast, a disc creation program for the Macintosh. Windows EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster. PowerISO. Mac Roxio Toast 20. CD or DVD disc image created by GEAR disc burning software. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. Recovery volume used by QuickPar, a parchive (parity archive) volume creating utility; contains a parity volume in PAR1 format; may be referenced to recover a file archive when one of the files in the archive is lost; also referenced to perform data integrity checks on files. To open a P19 file in QuickPar, click Open, navigate to your file, and click Open. Parchive recovery volumes are named with ascending number extensions, e.g., P01, P02, P03, P04, etc. NOTE: The.PAR and.PAR2 extensions are used for index files created with Parchive version 1 and 2 respectively. Windows QuickParFree.",
    "Windows EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster. PowerISO. Mac Roxio Toast 20. Windows GEAR Software GEAR PRO. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows QuickPar.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IXA",
    "Ulead Disc Image format",
    "3.0",
    "DVD disc image file created with Ulead disc authoring software; saved in a proprietary format readable only by Ulead applications, such as DVD Workshop, DVD Movie Factory, and Copy.Now. IXA files can be burned with the Ulead Disc Image Recorder, which is included with Ulead DVD PictureShow, VideoStudio, and MediaStudio Pro. Ulead is owned by the Corel Corporation. Windows Ulead MovieFactory or DVD Workshop Ulead Disc Image Recorder.",
    "Windows Ulead MovieFactory or DVD Workshop Ulead Disc Image Recorder.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".BWA",
    "BlindWrite Disk Information File",
    "3.0",
    "Game file created by Bookworm Adventures Deluxe, a spelling game; saves the players progress, including the number of chapters completed and spelling statistics; saved to the C:/​ProgramData/​PopCap Games/​Bookworm Adventures Deluxe/​WinBAD/​users/​ directory. Windows PopCap Games Bookworm Adventures DeluxeFree. Contains information about the physical characteristics of a CD or DVD; generated after scanning a disc with BWA Builder, a companion program for BlindWrite disc burning software; referenced when burning a copy of the disc. BlindWrite can blindly copy a disc without stopping due to disc errors. The BWA file reveals the severity of the errors on the disc. Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. VSO Software BWA Builder.",
    "Windows PopCap Games Bookworm Adventures Deluxe. Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. VSO Software BWA Builder.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".CL5",
    "Easy CD Creator 5 Saved Project",
    "3.0",
    "CD authoring project created with Easy CD Creator 5; contains references to files that will be included on the CD as well as other settings; may include data files or a playlist of audio files for creating an audio CD. NOTE: CL5 files cannot be opened with Roxios Easy Media Creator software. Windows Roxio Easy CD Creator 5.",
    "Windows Roxio Easy CD Creator 5.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SPARSEIMAGE",
    "Mac OS X Sparse Image",
    "3.0",
    "Disk image file type created with Apples Disk Utility program (included with Mac OS X); similar to a standard Apple Disk Image (.DMG) file, but only takes up the amount of space of the data contained within the disk image. To create a sparse image file, click the New Image button in Disk Utility. Then choose sparse disk image from the Image Format drop-down menu. NOTE: Apples FileVault feature initially used encrypted sparse image files to secure a users home directory. Newer versions of FileVault use the.SPARSEBUNDLE format. Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Apple DiskImageMounter.",
    "Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Apple DiskImageMounter.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".HDD",
    "Parallels Desktop Hard Disk",
    "3.0",
    "An HDD file is a virtual hard disk created by Parallels Desktop for Mac virtualization software. It stores a virtual machines hard drive data, including the programs and files on the virtual machine (VM). Parallels Desktop for Mac loads the HDD file when starting up a virtual machine. HDD file open in macOS Finder Parallels Desktop for Mac is an application that can run Windows, Linux, Android, and additional versions of macOS on Macs. When you create a virtual machine with the software, it creates a.PVM file, which is a macOS package that stores all of the files required to run the VM. One of the files created by Parallels Desktop for Mac and stored in the.PVM file is the HDD file. The HDD file functions similarly to Windows C:/​ drive. To view your HDD file, locate your PVM file, right-click it, and choose Show Package Contents. The HDD file is typically named after the operating system. For example, the HDD file for Windows 11 may be named Windows 11-0.hdd. NOTE: HDD files from version 2 of Parallels Desktop can be opened in Oracle VM VirtualBox. Other versions HDD files cannot be opened in VirtualBox. Typically, HDD files are opened as part of initializing a Parallels Desktop virtual machine. However, you can manually open and examine the files contained within a Windows HDD file by mounting the file to your macOS machine using Parallels Mounter, a program included with Parallels Desktop. After you mount your HDD file, youll be able to explore its contents in Finder. To mount an HDD file to your macOS machine: Locate your HDD file in Finder. Right-click the file and select Open With → Parallels Mounter. The volumes your HDD file contains will then appear in the Finder sidebar. Mac Parallels Desktop for Mac 18.",
    "Mac Parallels Desktop for Mac 18.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VCO",
    "Virtual CD Collection File",
    "3.0",
    "Disc image file created by Virtual CD, a program used for virtualizing physical discs as files on the hard drive; created by the softwares Collection Wizard and contains a grouping of virtual CDs; allows up to 255 virtual CDs to be inserted together as a single disc. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD.",
    "Windows H+H Software Virtual CD.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".WMT",
    "WinMount Virtual Disk File",
    "3.0",
    "Virtual disk or Ramdisk file created by WinMount, a compressed file management program; stores a virtual hard drive tha can be mounted by the program; includes the volume format (FAT32, NTFS, Unformatted) as well as the disk size. WMT files are used for mounting compressed archives to virtual disks. The archives can be in one of several formats, including.MOU,.ZIP,.ISO, and others. Windows WinMount International WinMountFree.",
    "Windows WinMount International WinMount.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".P2G",
    "Power2Go Project File",
    "3.0",
    "Disc image project created by Power2Go, a disc authoring and burning application; saved in an XML format and stores CD, DVD, or Blu-ray disc project settings; used for authoring audio, video, data, or mixed mode discs. P2G files allow users to save project settings and edit the disc project at a later time. They can be used for generating disc images in the proprietary.P2I format or in the standard.ISO format. Windows CyberLink Power2Go 13.",
    "Windows CyberLink Power2Go 13.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PQI",
    "PowerQuest Drive Image",
    "3.0",
    "Drive image created by PowerQuest disk imaging software; creates an exact copy or image of a hard drive used for backup purposes; can be used to restore a hard disk in case it crashes or fails. PQ! files can be opened with PowerQuests PQIBrowser (PQIBrowser.exe) or Symantec Norton Ghost (part of Norton SystemWorks). NOTE: PowerQuest was bought by Symantec and rebranded as Norton Ghost, which then became Ghost Solution Suite. Ghost saves backup files as.GHO files. Windows Symantec Ghost Solution Suite. PowerQuest PQIBrowser PowerQuest Drive Image.",
    "Windows Symantec Ghost Solution Suite. PowerQuest PQIBrowser PowerQuest Drive Image.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".HFV",
    "HFS Disk Image",
    "3.0",
    "Disk image used by emulation software, typically to run older versions of Mac operating systems; stores a disk image using Apples HFS file system format; used for running Mac OS in a Windows or Linux environment. Windows HFVExplorer. Mac SheepShaver. Linux SheepShaverFree.",
    "Windows HFVExplorer. Mac SheepShaver. Linux SheepShaver.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PGD",
    "PGP Disk Image",
    "3.0",
    "Encrypted disk image created by PGP, a data encryption program now owned by Symantec; saves data for a disk partition that can be mounted within a desktop environment; used for protecting sensitive files on the computer. PGP was originally developed by the PGP Corporation, which is now owned by Symantec. Several Symantec products now integrate the PGP software for encryption. Windows Symantec Drive Encryption —  (Discontinued). Trial Symantec PGP Desktop —  (Discontinued). Trial Mac Symantec Drive Encryption —  (Discontinued). Trial Symantec PGP Desktop —  (Discontinued). Trial Linux Symantec Drive Encryption —  (Discontinued). Trial. A PGD file is a video game created by Pocket Game Developer (PGD), a 2D game development app for Android. It is a Zip-compressed file that contains all the games source code and assets. Developers can export and import PGD files in Pocket Game Developer. Amateur game developers use Stray Pixel Games Pocket Game Developer to create simple 8-bit video games. They can then play these games in Pocket Game Developer and share them with other PGD users. To share a game with others, a developer must export the game as a PGD file. This file is a renamed ZIP archive that contains all the games data. To export a game as a PGD file, developers open the Game Settings menu (while editing their game), then select Export Game. You can open a PGD file in Pocket Game Developer (Android). To do so, select Import Games from the apps main menu. Then, navigate to and open your PGD file. If you want to view the constituent files a PGD file contains, you can do so by renaming the file to use the.ZIP extension, then expanding it as you would any ZIP file. All Platforms Rename.pgd to.zip; expand with any Zip-decompression utility Android Stray Pixel Games Pocket Game Developer.",
    "Windows Symantec Drive Encryption —  (Discontinued). Trial Symantec PGP Desktop —  (Discontinued). Trial. Mac Symantec Drive Encryption —  (Discontinued). Trial Symantec PGP Desktop —  (Discontinued). Trial. Linux Symantec Drive Encryption —  (Discontinued). Trial. All Platforms Rename.pgd to.zip; expand with any Zip-decompression utility. Android Stray Pixel Games Pocket Game Developer.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IMAGE",
    "Squeak Virtual Image",
    "3.0",
    "File containing exact copy of data from a Mac-formatted disk; can be mounted on the desktop like a CD or other disk; used by Disk Copy and ShrinkWrap. Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. Disk Copy ShrinkWrap. File used by OpenQuaq, a free virtual 3D online collaboration program; saves a base image that is used for running either the client or the server OpenQwaq application. Mac OpenQwaq. Windows OpenQwaq. Linux OpenQwaqFree. An IMAGE file is a virtual image that can be run in a Squeak virtual machine (VM). It contains a saved instance of a developers Squeak development session. IMAGE files can be used to create Squeak VMs on Windows, Mac, and Linux, as well as in web-based Squeak VMs. IMAGE file open in SqueakJS Squeak is a programming language and development environment that was created by developers at Apple and Disney. The Squeak language is based on the Smalltalk-80 language. Squeak is now open-source, meaning anyone can download the Squeak development environment and create programs using the Squeak programming language. The Squeak development environment comes packaged as an.EXE file,.DMG file, or other executable, which is used to launch a Squeak VM. In their VM, developers can develop programs using the Squeak programming language. When a developer saves a Squeak development session, the session is saved as an IMAGE file. A developer can load their IMAGE file the next time they start a Squeak VM, to resume their development session. You can open an IMAGE file in Squeak (cross-platform) or SqueakJS (Web). For example, to open an IMAGE file in SqueakJS, go to https://squeak.js.org/run/ and drag and drop your IMAGE file onto the page. Mac Squeak. Windows Squeak. Linux Squeak. Web SqueakJSFree.",
    "Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. Disk Copy ShrinkWrap. Mac OpenQwaq. Windows OpenQwaq. Linux OpenQwaq. Mac Squeak. Windows Squeak. Linux Squeak. Web SqueakJS.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VCX",
    "Virtual Disc Definition File",
    "3.0",
    "File created by Virtual CD Editor, a disc editing program included with the Virtual CD software; saves the list of files that will be burned to the disc; used for creating the disc image, which is comprised of a.VC4 file and.000, .001, .002, etc. data files. VCX files are used for saving editor projects and can be opened to view the list of files that are part of the disc image project. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Contains a table with references to classes (categories of objects) used by a Fox Pro database. Windows Microsoft Visual FoxPro.",
    "Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Windows Microsoft Visual FoxPro.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PMF",
    "Partition Image File",
    "3.0",
    "Read-only map file created with ArcMap, an application included with ESRI Desktop GIS; stores a constrained version of an.MXD map file; can be viewed with the free ArcReader application, which allows maps to be shared with users who have not purchased the commercial ArcGIS software; often emailed to clients or co-workers in collaborative office environments. PMF files can limit the data views, layouts, navigation, printing, querying, panning, and zooming capabilities of the map. They may also be password protected. This helps to protect the confidentiality and privacy of data. Published maps do not contain actual geographical data. Instead, they link to data sets at a central remote location, which may be hosted by an ArcMap Server or available over the Internet. Therefore, users need appropriate network access to the referenced data repository to view the maps. NOTE: PMF files are actually created by ArcGIS Publisher, an extension included with ArcMap. Windows ESRI ArcReader —  (Discontinued). ESRI ArcGIS Pro. A PMF file is a video saved in the PlayStation Movie Format (PSMF). It contains a movie, music video, video game cutscene, or other video meant to be played on the Sony PlayStation Portable (PSP). PMF file open in VLC media player The PlayStation Portable is a handheld gaming and entertainment console that Sony released in 2005. It allows users to download and play movies and other videos. These videos (and videos stored on PSP discs) are saved in the PSMF file format and most often use the.pmf extension. (Some PSMF videos instead use the.PMP extension). Users who want to extract PMF files from PSP disk images can do so using utilities such as UMDGen. They can then play the videos their PMF files contain on their PCs. You can open PMF files and play the videos they contain with VideoLAN VLC media player (multiplatform) or MPlayer (multiplatform). Windows File Viewer Plus — Get it from Microsoft. VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. FFmpeg. Mac VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. FFmpeg. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. FFmpeg. iOS VideoLAN VLC for Mobile. Android Videolabs VLC for AndroidFree. Partition image used by PartitionGuru, a partition management and data recovery program; stores a backup of a partition image that can be restored by the application when the current partition is damaged. To create a PMF file, select Tools in the menu, then click Clone Partition To Image File. Select the source partition, choose the image file, and click Start. To restore the partition image contained in the PMF file, select Tools in the menu, then click Restore Partition From Image File. Select the PMF image file, choose your target partition, and click Start.. Windows Eassos PartitionGuru.",
    "Windows ESRI ArcReader —  (Discontinued). ESRI ArcGIS Pro. Windows File Viewer Plus — Get it from Microsoft. VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. FFmpeg. Mac VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. FFmpeg. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player. MPlayer. FFmpeg. iOS VideoLAN VLC for Mobile. Android Videolabs VLC for Android. Windows Eassos PartitionGuru.",
    "You can use VideoLAN VLC media player to convert PMF files to other video and audio formats, including:.MP4 - MPEG-4 Video.WEBM - WebM Video.TS - Video Transport Stream.ASF - Advanced Systems Format.OGG - Ogg Vorbis Audio.MP3 - MP3 Audio.FLAC -. Lossless Audio Codec.WAV - WAVE Audio."],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".GI",
    "Global Image",
    "2.9",
    "Disk image format created by several possible disc creation software programs; used for duplicating CD/DVDs or copying disc information to a hard drive. Windows Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Lightning UK! ImgBurn. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Lightning UK! ImgBurn. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".NKIT",
    "Nintendo Kit Game ROM File",
    "2.9",
    "An NKIT file contains a complete copy of data extracted from a Nintendo Gamecube or Wii game saved in the Nintendo Toolkit (NKit) format. It is a compressed.ISO ROM file that reduces the excess and unnecessary data in a ROM file. NKIT files can be played on a computer with various emulation programs, such as Dolphin, Swiss, and Nintendont. The NKIT format was developed to reduce the size of ISO files that store an exact, uncompressed 1:1 copy of a Wii or Gamecube game, which can grow to be very large in size. You can convert ISO files to NKIT files with NKit. You can also convert NKIT files back to ISO files with NKit. NOTE: Downloading NKIT files containing Wii or Gamecube games is illegal. You should only dump Wii or Gamecube games from discs that you own. Windows NKit. Dolphin. Mac Dolphin. Linux DolphinFree.",
    "Windows NKit. Dolphin. Mac Dolphin. Linux Dolphin.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".NN",
    "Nero CD File List",
    "2.9",
    "List of files to be burned on a CD using the Nero disc authoring program; can be used to burn multiple discs with the same data; contains references to the files, but not the actual files themselves. Windows Nero 2022.",
    "Windows Nero 2022.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".RDF",
    "PowerProducer Disc Image",
    "2.9",
    "An RDF file is a document written in the Resource Description Framework (RDF) language, which is used to represent information about resources on the web. It contains information about a website in a structured format called metadata. RDF files may include a site map, an updates log, page descriptions, and keywords. RDF files are also used by Mozilla Web browsers, such as Firefox, to store settings for windows and toolbar configurations. Windows Mozilla Firefox. Mozilla SeaMonkey. Altova XMLSpy. SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Liquid Technologies Liquid XML Studio. Mac Mozilla Firefox. Mozilla SeaMonkey. SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Linux Mozilla Firefox. Mozilla SeaMonkey. SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Report file created by ArcGIS Desktop, an application suite for geospatial analysis and visualization; stores a generated report, which can include GIS statistics formatted as tabular data; generated from a report layout (.RLF) file. Windows ESRI ArcGIS Pro. Spreadsheet containing report output generated by a database or spreadsheet program such as Oracle or Microsoft Excel. Windows Microsoft Excel 365. Oracle Database. Mac Microsoft Excel 365. Lighting project created by Relux, a program used for modeling and simulating lighting conditions; can be an interior or exterior project; saves the building or room layout as well as light sources and their properties; can be visualized in the software to see if the lighting solution is suitable for construction. Windows ReluxSuiteFree. Disc image created with PowerProducer, a video capture and authoring program; contains all the content of a movie project, including special effects and settings; saved in a compressed format and may be burned to a disc for storage so it can be opened at a later time. Windows CyberLink PowerProducer 6. An RDF file is a template saved in the Research Documents Information Format (ReDIF). It contains metadata that describes one or more research publications, such as journals, papers, books, and software. RDF files store information about the publication, such as the author, email to contact the author, title of the publication, editor, date published, and workplace of the author of the publication. RDF files are used by Research Papers in Economics (RePEc), a group dedicated to enhance the dissemination of research in Economics and other related sciences. RePEc uses RDF and.REDIF files to store metadata about publications, which allows the publications to be more easily organized and accessed. RDF files are used to contain metadata with ISO-LATIN-1 or Windows-1252 character encoding, whereas REDIF files are used to store metadata with UTF-8 character encoding. RDF files are typically created with ReDIF software but since they are stored in plain text can be opened and edited by any text editor. Metadata stored in the RDF file is organized into separate lines of field_name: field value. An example of a field name and value is Author-Workplace-Name: University of Minnesota. Windows ReDIF. Any text editor Mac Any text editor Linux Any text editor. An RDF file is a reaction data file saved in a format created by Molecular Design Limited (MDL). It contains the same molecule data as an.SDF file and, typically, additional reaction data. Computational chemists export RDF files from ISIS, REACCS, and other chemical databases, for use in chemical analysis programs. Molecular Design Limited (also known as MDL Information Systems, before being acquired by Symyx in 2007) created several file formats that computational chemists use regularly. These include the SDF and RDF formats. Both the SDF and RDF formats save chemical data in a plain text format. RDF files include one or more records that typically document one or more reactant molecules and the product(s) of their reaction. Each record also contains a data block documenting the reaction itself. You can open an RDF file with CGRTools (multiplatform), Chemaxon Instant JChem (multiplatform), and other chemical and reaction analysis programs. Windows CGRTools. Chemaxon Instant JChem. Mac CGRTools. Chemaxon Instant JChem. Linux CGRTools. Chemaxon Instant JChem.",
    "Windows Mozilla Firefox. Mozilla SeaMonkey. Altova XMLSpy. SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Liquid Technologies Liquid XML Studio. Mac Mozilla Firefox. Mozilla SeaMonkey. SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Linux Mozilla Firefox. Mozilla SeaMonkey. SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Windows ESRI ArcGIS Pro. Windows Microsoft Excel 365. Oracle Database. Mac Microsoft Excel 365. Windows ReluxSuite. Windows CyberLink PowerProducer 6. Windows ReDIF. Any text editor. Mac Any text editor. Linux Any text editor. Windows CGRTools. Chemaxon Instant JChem. Mac CGRTools. Chemaxon Instant JChem. Linux CGRTools. Chemaxon Instant JChem.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PDI",
    "InstantCopy Disc Image",
    "2.8",
    "DVD disc image ripped from the original DVD using Pinnacle Systems InstantCopy DVD copying program. NOTE: Pinnacle Systems InstantCopy has been discontinued. Windows Pinnacle Systems InstantCopy Lightning UK! ImgBurn. Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "Windows Pinnacle Systems InstantCopy Lightning UK! ImgBurn. Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".WII",
    "Scrambled Wii Disc Image",
    "2.8",
    "Disc image format used by the homebrew community for Nintendo Wii discs; saves a Wii game in a scrambled format; can be unscrambled and converted to the.ISO format with Wii Unscrambler and then burned to a standard disc; sometimes used for backing up Wii games. The WII format is not supported by Nintendo, the developers of the Wii game console system. Windows Wii UnscramblerFree. A WII file is a game data archive used by a Nintendo Wii video game created using the Asura Engine. It contains game assets, such as in-game music and sound effects. The Wii version of The Simpsons Game contains WII files. The Asura Engine is a video game development engine created by Rebellion Developments. Many games published by Rebellion and other publishers, such as Electronic Arts, have been developed using the Asura Engine. Most games developed using the Asura Engine store their audio assets in.ASR files. However, the Wii versions of some games, such as The Simpsons Game, store these assets in WII files instead. WII files are not meant to be opened by players. Modders have yet to create tools for opening and extracting data from a games WII files. No programs available.",
    " Windows Wii Unscrambler. No programs available.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".DMS",
    "Amiga Disk Masher Image",
    "2.8",
    "A DMS file is a compressed Amiga disk image created using Amigas Disk Masher System (DMS). It contains data intended for use with an Amiga computer. DMS files were originally used to transfer data among Amiga users via physical floppy disks, but are now most often used to share Amiga games, programs, and other files online, for use with Amiga emulators such as Amiga Forever. The Amiga was created by Commodore International and was one of the earliest PCs to support advanced graphics, sound, and video. Its superior multimedia capabilities made it quite popular among gamers, music enthusiasts, video producers, and other creatives. However, as other companies released computers and game consoles that matched or surpassed the Amigas capabilities, the Amiga faded from the PC market. Many modern decompression programs do not support the DMS format, so you might have to download a program such as The Unarchiver (Mac) or WinFellow (Windows) if you want to view the files contained within a DMS archive. To actually open and use the files contained within a DMS archive, you will have to download an Amiga emulator such as Amiga Forever. Windows Cloanto Amiga Forever. WinFellow. WinUAE. Mac The Unarchiver. Cloanto Amiga Forever. MaxUAEFree. A DMS file is a script file (i.e. connect.dms) run by Dream Seeker, a client for playing online games. It was created by Dream Maker, part of the BYOND (Build Your Own Net Dream) suite of programs. Windows BYOND. Mac BYOND. Linux BYONDFree. A DMS file is a file that has been downloaded and renamed by the Apple Safari web browser or the Apple Mail email client. It contains the contents of a file that did not have a file extension or was erroneously renamed when downloaded. The.dms extension is a result of a bug in Safari and Mail. You most likely will encounter this type of DMS file only if you download a file that does not have a file extension in macOS with Safari or Mail. In some cases, Safari or Mail may also replace a valid extension with.dms or may append the.dms extension onto a file extension. For example, sample.pdf may become sample.dms or sample.pdf.dms when downloaded with Safari or Mail. The bug is specific to Macs and has been reported across multiple versions of Mac operating systems, with some issues dating back to the early 2000s. While Safari and Mail are the typical perpetrators, the.dms renaming may also occur with other web browsers and programs. To open a DMS file, rename the.dms extension with the appropriate extension for the file type. For example, if you know you downloaded an.XLSX file that was renamed with the.dms extension, click on the extension to highlight it and change it back to.xlsx. Then, you can open the file with a program that supports XLSX files. Windows Rename the.dms extension and open it with the appropriate program Mac Rename the.dms extension and open it with the appropriate program Linux Rename the.dms extension and open it with the appropriate program.",
    "Windows Cloanto Amiga Forever. WinFellow. WinUAE. Mac The Unarchiver. Cloanto Amiga Forever. MaxUAE. Windows BYOND. Mac BYOND. Linux BYOND. Windows Rename the.dms extension and open it with the appropriate program. Mac Rename the.dms extension and open it with the appropriate program. Linux Rename the.dms extension and open it with the appropriate program.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".EDA",
    "Ensoniq ASR Disk Image",
    "2.8",
    "An EDA file contains a copy of a disk image formatted for the Ensoniq Advanced Sampling Recorder (ASR) digital music production studio. It may store an instrument, sound bank, sequence, song, or effects dumped from a floppy disk. Ensoniq released the ASR in 1992 and continued manufacturing it until 1998. It followed the Ensoniq EPS and Ensoniq EPS-16+ samplers and included a floppy drive. Users could add sounds, presets, and songs to the sampler via floppy disks and share sounds with other users that they created with the ASR. While Ensoniq no longer produces the sampler, some music enthusiasts dump the data from the floppy disks and save them in the EDA format. Then, they play the EDA files or convert them to other file types with software. You can open an EDA file with Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools in Windows. Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools. Chicken Systems Translator.",
    "Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools. Chicken Systems Translator.",
    "Ensoniq Disk Tools and Chicken Systems Translator can convert EDA files to several formats. For example, Ensoniq Disk Tools can convert EDA files to the following file types:.WAV - WAVE Audio File.MIDI - MIDI Music Data.SMF - Standard MIDI File.AIFF - Audio Interchange File Format."],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".TD0",
    "Teledisk Archive",
    "2.8",
    "A TD0 file is a disk archive created by the Teledisk archival tool. Teledisk was originally used for backing up files onto 3.5 inch floppy disks. The program was first developed by Sydex, then by New Technologies, and later discontinued. Windows Sydex Teledisk PUTR. A TD0 file is a collection of sounds usable by an Akai sampler. It is saved in Teledisk format. Windows Sydex Teledisk wteledsk.",
    "Windows Sydex Teledisk PUTR. Windows Sydex Teledisk wteledsk.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IBQ",
    "IsoBuster Managed Image File",
    "2.8",
    "Disc image format that enables disc image extraction to be stopped and started again later, even using different equipment; provides redundancy for reading over disc errors. Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".DXP",
    "CDBurnerXP Data Compilation File",
    "2.7",
    "Data disc compilation file created by CDBurnerXP, a program used to burn data and music discs; contains a list of files and directories to burn, as well as the file sizes and the target directory structure; saved in an XML format. NOTE: CDBurnerXP stores audio disc compilation files with the.AXP extension. Windows Canneverbe CDBurnerXPFree. Settings file created by Dexpot, a Windows program that allows multiple virtual desktops to be used at the same time; saves settings such as the number of desktops, desktop behaviors, and hotkeys for switching between desktops. DXP files can be opened and saved through Dexpots Settings window. However, the user doesnt specify the directory when saving profiles. Instead, Dexpot automatically saves them to the following directory: [user]/​AppData/​Roaming/​Dexpot/​profile/​. Windows Dexpot. File created by Duxbury Braille Translator, a braille translator; contains Print that can be translated into braille; may contain other document elements such as tables. To create a DXP file with the Duxbury Braille Translator, select File → Save or Save as.., name the file, choose the save location, and click Save. To open a DXP file with the Duxbury Braille Translator, select File → Open.., navigate to your file, and click Open. NOTE: Similar to the.DXB file extension that contains braille instead of Print. Duxbury MegaDots has been discontinued. Windows Duxbury Braille Translator. Duxbury MegaDots —  (Discontinued). Trial.",
    "Windows Canneverbe CDBurnerXP. Windows Dexpot. Windows Duxbury Braille Translator. Duxbury MegaDots —  (Discontinued). Trial.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".WBI",
    "Compact ISO File",
    "2.7",
    "Compact ISO file used to store Wii game data; contains an exact duplicate of data from the original disc, but does not include any junk data.. WBI files are saved in the CISO format, which is a CD or DVD disc image image container format designed for efficient storage and distribution of CD/DVD images. The WBI file is the same as an.ISO file except it is compact, which means it removes all the junk data used to pad Wii games to make them appear as full discs. WBI files may also be encrypted and password protected or digitally signed for software distribution. NOTE: CISO files are more commonly seen with the.CISO file extension. Windows Wii Backup ManagerFree.",
    "Windows Wii Backup Manager.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".FDI",
    "Amiga Formatted Disk Image",
    "2.7",
    "An FDI file is a disk image created for use with an Amiga computer or emulator. It typically contains a copy of an Amiga application, such as a video game. Users can load FDI files on an Amiga or with an Amiga emulator to access the data they contain. Commodore produced the Amiga line of computers in the 1980s and 90s. Amiga users created floppy disk images saved in various formats, including the.ADF,.ADZ, and.DMS formats. In 2000, developer Vincent Joguin created the FDI disk image format, to make sharing data between Amiga users easier. The FDI format is open-source, so most Amiga emulators can mount FDI disk images and access the data they contain. Also, unlike other Amiga disk images, FDI files can store raw low-level data, such as data required to support copy protection schemes. As such, FDI files are often larger than other Amiga disk images. You can open an FDI file with Amiga emulators such as Cloanto Amiga Forever (multiplatform), WinUAE (Windows), and E-UAE (Mac, Linux). Windows WinUAE. WinFellow. Cloanto Amiga Forever. Mac E-UAE. Cloanto Amiga Forever. Linux E-UAEFree.",
    "Windows WinUAE. WinFellow. Cloanto Amiga Forever. Mac E-UAE. Cloanto Amiga Forever. Linux E-UAE.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".GDRIVE",
    "Gizmo Virtual Drive File",
    "2.7",
    "File created by Gizmo Manager, a virtual drive creation program included with the Gizmo Central software package; stores a password protected disk image in a proprietary format; used for storing sensitive data. Windows Arainia Solutions Gizmo CentralFree.",
    "Windows Arainia Solutions Gizmo Central.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".CD",
    "CD-i OptImage",
    "2.6",
    "Developer file created by Visual Studio, a software development application; stores a diagram of classes and helps developers visualize the structure of source code; created by the Class Diagram editor in Visual Studio. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Copy of a CD saved in the CD-i disc image format. Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".86F",
    "86Box Floppy Disk Image ",
    "2.6",
    "An 86F file is a disk image used by 86Box, a fork of the PCem emulator that emulates PC hardware from the 1980s and 90s. It is saved in the 86Box Floppy Disk (86F) format, which is designed to replicate actual floppy disk images. 86F files may contain an application or a game. The 86Box software allows a user to install and run a variety of operating systems, such as DOS, Windows 1, Windows 95, and Windows XP. The 86F format was developed specifically for 86Box as an alternative to the.IMG format in order to better handle the various disk image formats associated with different operating systems. NOTE: 86Box and the 86F format are developed by Miran Grca (Battler). Windows 86BoxFree.",
    "Windows 86Box.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".B5T",
    "BlindWrite 5 Disc Track Information File",
    "2.6",
    "Contains descriptor information for a disk created by BlindWrite; typically saved with a corresponding.B5I file; relatively small compared to the.B5I file. Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".B6T",
    "BlindWrite 6 Track Information File",
    "2.6",
    "Information file created by BlindWrite 6, a game software backup program; contains track information for a disc image generated by BlindWrite. B6T files only contain disc track information. Each B6T file is saved along a.B6I file, which contains the actual data stored in the disc image. Opening a B6T file will mount the corresponding BlindWrite disc image (B6I file). NOTE: BlindWrite 5 saved track information files as.B5I files. Previous versions of BlindWrite used the.BWT file extension. Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows VSO Software BlindWrite. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SIMG",
    "Synclavier Disk Image File",
    "2.5",
    "An SIMG file is a disk image file saved in the proprietary Synclavier Disk Image (SIMG) format. It stores a collection of sounds used to compose music with SynclavierX, a digital synthesizer program. SIMG files are designed to be mounted by SynclavierX to allow users to access all their songs in one file without having to load multiple files. You most likely will only encounter an SIMG file if you use SynclavierX software. However, SynclavierX has been discontinued and SIMG files are no longer used, making them uncommon. NOTE: SynclavierX is a program that emulates the capabilities of a Synclavier digital synthesizer, which was a physical music workstation manufactured from the 1970s to the 1990s. Mac Synclavier Digital SynclavierX.",
    "Mac Synclavier Digital SynclavierX.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".EDV",
    "Ensoniq VFX-SD Disk Image",
    "2.5",
    "An EDV file is a disk image formatted for an Ensoniq VFX-SD, a low-cost, high-performance workstation synthesizer released in 1989. It stores data dumped from a floppy disk, including individual sounds, sets of sounds, banks of sounds, and presets. Ensoniq released the VFX-SD keyboard in 1989. It improved upon the VFX synthesizer Ensoniq released earlier that year. Users could insert floppy disks into the VFX-SD floppy drive to add sounds, presets, and songs to the synthesizer. They could also install operating system updates via the floppy disk. EDV Disk Image Format The Ensoniq VFX-SD disk image format is similar to the EPS, EPS-16 PLUS, and SD-1 devices but with different directory information. In addition, the format features 160 tracks (80 on each side of the disk), with each track storing 512-byte sectors of data. You can open EDV files with Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools in Windows. Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "Ensoniq Disk Tools can convert EDV files to several file types, including the following formats:.WAV - WAVE Audio File.AIFF - Audio Interchange File Format.MIDI - MIDI Music Data.SMF - Standard MIDI File."],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".ATR",
    "Atari Disk Image",
    "2.4",
    "Disk image created on an Atari computer system; contains an exact copy of an Atari 8-bit formatted disk; may be a single or double density image containing 720 or 1440 sectors. ATR files can be mounted on a PC using an Atari 8-bit computer emulation program like PC Xformer, APE, or SIO2PC. Windows PC Xformer Classic PC Xformer 2000 APE SIO2PC.",
    "Windows PC Xformer Classic PC Xformer 2000 APE SIO2PC.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IBB",
    "ImgBurn Project File",
    "2.4",
    "Disk burning project created by ImgBurn, a program used for burning data, audio, and video discs; saves a list of files, folders, and/or disk images to be burned to a disc; can be used for CDs, DVDs, HD DVDs, and Blu-ray discs. Windows Lightning UK! ImgBurnFree.",
    "Windows Lightning UK! ImgBurn.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".C2D",
    "WinOnCD Disc Image",
    "2.4",
    "Copy of a CD created by WinOnCD, a disc imaging program created by Sonic Solutions and later published by Roxio; stores an exact copy of the data from a CD and may be used for duplicating or backing up discs. Windows Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial Mac Roxio Toast 20.",
    "Windows Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. MagicISO —  (Discontinued). Trial. Mac Roxio Toast 20.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".WINCLONE",
    "Winclone Image",
    "2.4",
    "Disk image file created by Winclone, a program used for managing Mac Boot Camp partitions; often contains an image of a Windows partition for XP, Vista, or 7; allows a Boot Camp partition to be saved as an image and then restored on another drive. Mac Twocanoes Winclone.",
    "Mac Twocanoes Winclone.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VOLARCHIVE",
    "CopyCatX Volume Archive",
    "2.4",
    "Disk image file created by CopyCatX, a hard drive duplication program for Mac OS X; saves a copy of the hard drive but doesnt save empty sectors so that the file doesnt become unecessarily large; used for backup and restore operations and for duplicating boot drives. Mac SubRosaSoft.com CopyCatX.",
    "Mac SubRosaSoft.com CopyCatX.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MINISO",
    "Mini Image Destination File",
    "2.4",
    "Image file format used by DVDFab Blu-ray Copy, a program that enables users to copy Blu-ray content to a Blu-ray, DVD, and AVCHD disc; similar to an.ISO file but much smaller in size. The MINISO format allows you to play Blu-ray folders and files. When you copy a Blu-ray disc or folder to a folder on your hard drive, the MINISO file is created. If you double-click the file it will be mounted and ready for use, however you must keep the source folder for the MINISO file on your hard disk. Windows DVDFab Blu-ray Copy. Lightning UK! ImgBurn. Mac DVDFab Blu-ray Copy.",
    "Windows DVDFab Blu-ray Copy. Lightning UK! ImgBurn. Mac DVDFab Blu-ray Copy.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".LNX",
    "Atari Lynx ROM",
    "2.3",
    "An LNX file is an archive that contains one or more files that comprise a Commodore 64 game or program. It is packaged with the Lynx archiver program and is not compressed. Commodore 64 users often archived.D64 or.X64 files and any related files to upload to bulletin board systems (BBS) to share them with other computer enthusiasts. Will Corley developed the Lynx (also known as LNX or Ultimate Lynx) format. The Lynx format was more common in the 1990s for uploading to a BBS but has since greatly decreased in usage due to the rise of other archiving formats, discontinuation of the Lynx archiver program, and the complexity of the Lynx format. You can open an LNX file with the VICE and WinVICE emulators. Windows WinVICE. Mac VICEFree. An LNX file is a ROM image of an Atari Lynx video game. It contains the complete data of an Atari Lynx game dumped from a physical game cartridge. Retro gamers use LNX files to store games they can load and play with an Atari Lynx emulation application on a computer. Atari Lynx is a handheld game console released in 1989. It featured an LCD color screen, 64 KB RAM, and support for various games, such as Ms. Pac-Man, Lemmings, Paperboy, and Desert Strike. Lynx gaming enthusiasts utilize emulation software to play games on their computers without the Lynx gaming console. For example, nostalgic gamers who enjoyed the Lynx console in the 1990s use emulation software, such as Handy Emulator and Mednafen, to emulate Atari Lynx game ROMs. Before being able to play a Lynx game with emulation software, gamers must dump game ROM data from a Lynx game cartridge onto their computer and save them as an LNX file. After saving them in the Lynx game ROM format, they can load and play the game saved as the LNX file with an emulator. You can open an LNX file with several Atari Lynx emulators, such as Handy Emulator (Windows and macOS) and Mednafen (Windows). Windows Handy Emulator. Mednafen. Mac Handy EmulatorFree.",
    "Windows WinVICE. Mac VICE. Windows Handy Emulator. Mednafen. Mac Handy Emulator.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SOPT",
    "Synclavier Optical Disk Image File",
    "2.3",
    "Custom format for Synclavier optical disk image files. Mac Synclavier Digital SynclavierX.",
    "Mac Synclavier Digital SynclavierX.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".ADF",
    "Archimedes Disk File",
    "2.3",
    "An ADF file is a database file created by ACT!, a customer relationship management (CRM) application. It contains customer contact data such as names, email addresses, business information, and the relationship history. ADF files are used for customer relations information in a structured format. ADF files are saved with an.ALF lookup file, which contains metadata required to navigate, backup, and restore the ACT! database. ADF files may also be archived within ACT! database backups, which use the.BAK extension. ADF files use Microsoft SQL Server formatting and are similar to.MDF files. NOTE: Sage ACT! became Swiftpage Act! in 2013. Windows Swiftpage Act!. An ADF file is a disk image created on an Amiga system. It stores an exact copy of an Amiga-formatted disk in fixed-width AmigaDOS data tracks. ADF files can be mounted as a physical disk using an Amiga emulation program. ADF files can be created on a Windows PC using ADF Opus ane opened using several other programs, such as WinUAE, WinFellow, and Cloanto Amiga Forever. You can install the unADF tool on a Linux Debian operating system (OS) by typing the following command in terminal: #sudo apt-get install unadf. Windows Cloanto Amiga Forever. ADF Opus. WinUAE. WinFellow. Mac Incredible Bee Archiver. Cloanto Amiga Forever. MaxUAE. E-UAE. The Unarchiver. Linux unADF. E-UAEFree. Internal raster data format used by ESRI products such as ArcGIS, ArcView, and ArcInfo Workstation; stores spatial data as a binary grid (rows and columns of cells) and is one of several ADF files that together comprise the total grid; used to represent geographical or other spatial objects such as maps and map features. An ArcInfo binary grid can contain multiple raster layers, forming a layer stack. This can be used to overlay different views on surfaces and perform statistical analysis on the spatial data. ADF files can store metadata about the raster set or actual raster data. When you create a binary grid, the files named dblbnd.adf, hdr.adf, sta.adf, vat.adf, and w001001x.adf provide metadata about the raster grid, while the file w001001.adf contains the actual raster data. These files are typically separated out into their own folder so that they are easy to manage as an entire grid coverage set. Windows ESRI ArcGIS Pro. TatukGIS Editor. GDALFree. An ADF file contains audio data used by Grand Theft Auto: Vice City (GTAVC), a video game where players steal cars and complete missions. It stores music or radio DJ interviews played by a specific radio station that can be tuned into and played when driving a vehicle during gameplay. You most likely will only encounter GTAVC ADF files if you are looking in the game installation directory to modify the music played by the radio station in the game or convert the songs to a different, more widely supported audio format. There is one ADF file for each radio station. ADF files are not meant to be manually opened. Instead, they are referenced by the GTAVC game. However, gamers looking to convert ADF files can use the Vice City Audio Converter utility developed by Raptor to convert them to.MP3 audio files. Windows Rockstar Games Grand Theft Auto: Vice City. Raptor Vice City Audio ConverterFree. An ADF file is a document created by Software AG ARIS Express, a free program used to model business process analysis and management. It contains a diagram, which consists of symbols used to model and organize a business-related process. ADF file open in Software AG ARIS Express 2.4 ADF files may be used to store a variety of model types, such as organizational charts, data models, whiteboards, system landscapes, process landscapes, business processes, and IT infrastructures. You can choose from a variety of symbols to best organize the diagram. Some of the symbols that can be added are events, tasks, subprocesses, data objects, messages, text annotations, connections, and gateways. The ADF file is saved in a proprietary format and is the primary file type used to export diagrams in ARIS Express. However, the program can also export diagrams to other formats, including.JPEG,.PNG,.PDF, and.EMF. To export an ADF file to one of these formats, select File → Export and choose the format. To create an ADF file, select File → New.., choose the model type, then select File → Save. To open an ADF file, select File → Open.. and navigate to the location of the ADF file. You can also choose File → Model history to open a recently opened model. Windows Software AG ARIS Express. Mac Software AG ARIS Express. Linux Software AG ARIS ExpressFree. An ADF file is a disk image used by the Archimedes system included on Acorn Archimedes home computers during the 1980s and 1990s. It contains an exact copy of a physical Archimedes-formatted floppy disk, which was used to distribute software, such as games or other programs. The Archimedes family of computers were discontinued in the mid-1990s. But if you owned an Archimedes computer or are just a curious retro techie, you may want to mount an ADF file with an Archimedes emulator to use the software. There are several Archimedes emulators available that can mount an ADF file. Some examples are Arculator, ArcEm, and Archie. Windows Arculator. ArcEm. Archie. Mac ArcEmFree. An ADF file contains a webpage design created by PY Software Actual Drawing, a visual web authoring tool. It stores a drawing of a webpage, which includes the structure of the webpage, along with colors, shapes, images, text boxes, tables, and animations included on the page. ADF files can be exported to.HTML files for publishing online when completed. You will most likely only encounter Actual Drawing ADF files if you used Actual Drawing to create a website in the early 2000s. Since Actual Drawing is no longer developed and its features are outdated, ADF files are uncommon. If you find an ADF file and need to open it, you can open it with Actual Drawing by selecting File → Open.. You can then export the design to an HTML file by selecting File → Export to HTML file... Windows PY Software Actual Drawing.",
    "Windows Swiftpage Act!. Windows Cloanto Amiga Forever. ADF Opus. WinUAE. WinFellow. Mac Incredible Bee Archiver. Cloanto Amiga Forever. MaxUAE. E-UAE. The Unarchiver. Linux unADF. E-UAE. Windows ESRI ArcGIS Pro. TatukGIS Editor. GDAL. Windows Rockstar Games Grand Theft Auto: Vice City. Raptor Vice City Audio Converter. Windows Software AG ARIS Express. Mac Software AG ARIS Express. Linux Software AG ARIS Express. Windows Arculator. ArcEm. Archie. Mac ArcEm. Windows PY Software Actual Drawing.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".SPARSEBUNDLE",
    "Mac OS X Sparse Bundle",
    "2.3",
    "Macintosh disk image format introduced in Mac OS X 10.5 Leopard; based off the.SPARSEIMAGE format, but is made up of bands instead of individual files; contains mountable directories saved in 8MB chunks. To create a new sparse bundle image file, click the New Image button in Disk Utility. Then select sparse bundle disk image from the Image Format drop-down menu. NOTE: In Mac OS 10.5, both FileVault and Time Machine use the SPARSEBUNDLE format. FileVault uses encrypted sparse bundles to secure the home directory. Time Machine creates sparse bundles and only backs up the 8MB chunks that have changed since the last backup. Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Apple DiskImageMounter.",
    "Mac Apple Disk Utility Included with OS. Apple DiskImageMounter.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".G41",
    "Commodore 1541 Disk Image",
    "2.3",
    "Disk image file used by Commodore 64 emulation software; saves data in the same format as a floppy disk used by the Commodore 1541 floppy disk drive, which supported 5.25 disks and was used with some older Commodore 64 systems; uses GCR (Group Code Recording) methods to save the data. G41 files can be used for running older Commodore 64 games and other programs using Commodore emulation software such as CCS64. Windows CCS64. WinVICE. Mac VICEFree.",
    "Windows CCS64. WinVICE. Mac VICE.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".NCD",
    "NTI CD-Maker Disc Image",
    "2.3",
    "Disc artwork created by Nero CoverDesigner, CD or DVD label designer included with the Nero Multimedia Suite; stores the text, graphics, and layout dimensions for the design; can be printed to a disc using a compatible printer. In addition to disc labels, NCD files can also be used to store artwork documents for CD or DVD booklet inserts. NOTE: Nero CoverDesigner is part of Nero 6.6 or later. Windows Nero 2022. DVD or CD disc image created with NTI (formerly NewTech Infosystems) disc burning and backup software. Windows NTI Media Maker Express. EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster. Mac NTI Dragon Burn 4.",
    "Windows Nero 2022. Windows NTI Media Maker Express. EZB Systems UltraISO. Smart Projects IsoBuster. Mac NTI Dragon Burn 4.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".TAO",
    "Track at Once CD/DVD Image",
    "2.3",
    "Copy of a disc created with CD/DVD Duplicator software; used by CD and DVD duplicator systems that can duplicate several discs at a time. Windows Iopshop Duplicator software Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows Iopshop Duplicator software Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".XMD",
    "Extended Media Disc Image",
    "2.3",
    "File used by Bitdefender antivirus and Internet protection software; saves file signature information that helps the software recognize threats for common file exploitations, such as.ZIP and.RAR. Windows Bitdefender Total Security. Bitdefender Antivirus Plus. Mac Bitdefender Antivirus for Mac. Disc image file originally created by GameJack, a program used for backing up games on CDs and DVDs; contains an exact copy of the raw data on the disc and enables exact copies of discs to be made; also supports label making and disc protection scanning options. GameJack was been replaced by Engelmann Media 5-Star GameCopy. The XMD format is now supported by other disc imaging software such as Engelmann CDRWIN and H+H Softwares Virtual CD software. Windows Engelmann Media CDRWIN —  (Discontinued). H+H Software Virtual CD. EZB Systems UltraISO. Disc4You GameJack. Answer file used by ExamSoft, an online assessment-management program; contains answers from an exam administered through ExamSoft. The XMD file is created by SofTest, an ExamSoft application for Windows and OS X. When completing an exam using SofTest, an XMD file is generated to contain the answers. ExamSoft is an online tool that allows you to control the entire testing process. It enables you to create exams, and provides delivery, scoring, administration, and analysis features. It is commonly used in the K-12, undergrad, nursing, medical, pharmacy, law, and dental fields of study. NOTE: The XMD file was replaced by the.XMDX file format. Web ExamSoft.",
    "Windows Bitdefender Total Security. Bitdefender Antivirus Plus. Mac Bitdefender Antivirus for Mac. Windows Engelmann Media CDRWIN —  (Discontinued). H+H Software Virtual CD. EZB Systems UltraISO. Disc4You GameJack. Web ExamSoft.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".P2I",
    "Power2Go Disc Image",
    "2.2",
    "Disc image file created by Power2Go, a CD, DVD, and Blu-ray disc burning application; stores the full contents of the target disc image in a proprietary format; can be used for burning multiple discs with the same contents; can also be browsed in the software. NOTE: Power2Go can also write disc images to the.ISO format. Windows CyberLink Power2Go 13.",
    "Windows CyberLink Power2Go 13.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".FCD",
    "Virtual CD Format",
    "2.2",
    "Uncompressed image of a CD-ROM created by Virtual CD versions 1 through 3; supported in later versions of Virtual CD, but saved in a different format. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. Database created by First Choice, an early productivity suite from SoftKey International. Windows SoftKey PFS:First Choice. 2D vector drawing created with FastCAD for DOS, a CAD software program; contains objects that are used to represent architectural designs and layouts. Newer versions of FastCAD save files with a.FCW extension. Mac IMSI TurboCAD Deluxe. Windows Evolution Computing FastCAD IMSI TurboCAD Pro.",
    "Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO. Windows SoftKey PFS:First Choice. Mac IMSI TurboCAD Deluxe. Windows Evolution Computing FastCAD IMSI TurboCAD Pro.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".MIR",
    "Mini-Image Ripper Disc Image",
    "2.2",
    "Disc Image created by Mini-Image Ripper (MIR), a program that converts SafeDisc game discs into small disc images; stores the game data and excludes the data on the disc not used specifically for the game; creates a mini disc about 23MB in size. MIR disc images may work for some SafeDisc discs, but the Mini-Image Ripper documentation states that it will not work for all games stored in the SafeDisc format. Some MIR files can be opened with DAEMON Tools, but the program does not include the MIR format in its supported format list. NOTE: SafeDisc is a disc copy DRM protection format created by Macrovision. Windows DT Soft DAEMON Tools. Mini-Image RipperFree.",
    "Windows DT Soft DAEMON Tools. Mini-Image Ripper.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".NFI",
    "Dreambox Disc Image",
    "2.0",
    "An NFI file is a data file created by the Netflix streaming app for Android and iOS. It contains information that describes a movie or show downloaded using the app. NFI files are saved to the device in a folder along with several other files, which include the.MANIFEST,.NFV,.NFA, and.NFS files. The Netflix app allows Android and iOS users to download a wide variety of movies and shows for viewing when not connected to the Internet. Most titles on Netflix are available for download although some are excluded. If a title can be downloaded it has a download icon next to the name. You can search for downloadable titles by selecting Available for Download from the Netflix menu. Tap the download icon to begin the download then navigate to the My Downloads section of the app to access the downloaded movie or show. If you want to delete a movie or show you downloaded to your Android device tap the menu icon in the app, select My Downloads, tap the pencil edit icon in the upper right corner, and tap the red X icon to delete the title. If you want to delete a movie or show you downloaded to your iOS device tap the menu icon in the app, select My Downloads, tap Edit, and select the red X icon to delete the title. NOTE: In order to download Netflix content on your Android or iOS app you need to have Android 4.4.2 or later or iOS 8.0 or later. You also need the latest version of the Netflix app. iOS Netflix. Android Netflix. Disc image created from the hard drive of a Dreambox satellite or cable television receiver; serves as a backup file that can be used to restore data to a Dreambox. NFI files distributed by Dream Multimedia may also be used to update the firmware of Dreambox DVRs. Windows FlashWizard FlashWizard PRO.",
    "iOS Netflix. Android Netflix. Windows FlashWizard FlashWizard PRO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".WIL",
    "WinImage Disk Image List",
    "2.0",
    "List of disk images generated by WinImage, a disk imaging program; created using the Batch Assistant within WinImage; may be used to export multiple.IMA files or a single.WLZ file. A WIL file can also be imported into WinImage to load a set of disk images into the Batch Assistant. Windows Gilles Vollant WinImage.",
    "Windows Gilles Vollant WinImage.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".EDQ",
    "Ensoniq SQ1/SQ2/KS32 Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "Copy of a disk formatted for an Ensoniq SQ1, SQ2, or KS32 music keyboard; can be copied to a disk and loaded on an Ensoniq keyboard; may also be opened and read on a PC using a utility like Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools. Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".RATDVD",
    "RatDVD Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "Compressed DVD disk image stored in the ratDVD container format; can reduce the size of a 9 GB video DVD to 1-3 GB; maintains full picture quality, multiple audio channels, video angles, etc; also saves multiple versions of the movie, special features, complete menus, and navigation features. Features from ratDVD are now available in fluxDVD, a commercial DVD burning and playback program. Windows ratDVD ACE GmbH fluxDVD.",
    "Windows ratDVD ACE GmbH fluxDVD.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PGX",
    "Hopedot VOS Data File",
    "2.0",
    "File created by Hopedot VOS, a thin virtualization client used for running Windows programs from an external USB device; saves all applications and files on an external drive partition and encrypts them so that they cannot be accessed without valid login information. When installing Hopedot VOS, you must first specify a portable drive that stores the Windows applications and files. Hopedot VOS then creates a PGX file on the drive to store the data. NOTE: Hopedot VOS is discontinued. Windows Hopedot VOS.",
    "Windows Hopedot VOS.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".HD",
    "IBochs Virtual Hard Disk",
    "2.0",
    "An HD file is a virtual hard disk used by the IBochs PC Emulator for Android. IBochs users can write data to this file just as they would a real hard disk. IBochs comes with one included HD file, named LBOCHS.HD. IBochs uses LBOCHS.HD to save and load users data. For example, users can write an operating system to LBOCHS.HD and then load that operating system from IBochss boot menu. Users can also load operating systems from virtual floppy disks (.FD files) and CD-ROMs. LBOCHS.HD is not meant to be opened. If your copy of LBOCHS.HD contains an operating system, you can use the file to load the operating system in IBochs PC Emulator (Android). To do so: Make sure your LBOCHS.HD file is stored in ~/​Android/​data/​lb.myapp.lbochs/​files. Open IBochs and press F12 to access the boot menu. Select 2. Hard Disk. The operating system your HD file contains will then load in IBochs. Android IBochs PC EmulatorFree.",
    "Android IBochs PC Emulator.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".DO",
    "DOS 3.3 Order Apple II Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "A DO file is a web-based Java program run by a web server that supports Java, such as Tomcat or IBM WebSphere. It is typically mapped to the Struts controller, which processes the file. DO files are used for generating dynamic webpages. Some webinar programs may generate DO files that are formatted as.VCS files. Changing the file extension to .vcs may allow these files to be imported into various scheduling programs. NOTE: The .do extension may also be a URL mapping scheme for a web application and not a file extension. For example, the Struts framework often uses the .do string for mapping Java servlet actions in the web.xml configuration file. Windows Apache Tomcat. Any web browser Mac Apache Tomcat. Any web browser Linux Apache Tomcat. Any web browser. A DO file is a disk image created for use with an Apple II computer. It contains data that was imaged from an Apple IIs hard disk, for use as a backup or historical archive. DO files are saved using DOS 3.3 sector order, as opposed to ProDOS sector order. DO file open in CiderPress Various tools allow Apple II series users to save the contents of their computers hard disks as disk images. These images can include applications, files, and other data stored on the computers. Users can save Apple II disk images in many different formats. Most commonly, Apple II disk images are saved as.DSK files. However, the images may also be saved as DO files or.PO files. DO files store data in DOS 3.3 sector order; PO files store data in ProDOS sector order. (Some Apple II computers used the DOS 3.3 operating systems, while others used ProDOS.). You can open a DO file in several Apple II disk image utilities and Apple II emulators. For example: CiderPress (Windows) allows you to open a DO file to examine, extract, or add to its contents Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS (multiplatform) allows you to transfer DO files to an Apple II computer. KEGS (Kents Emulated GS) (multiplatform) allows you to open and mount DO files. Windows CiderPress. Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Mac Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Linux Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGSFree.",
    "Windows Apache Tomcat. Any web browser. Mac Apache Tomcat. Any web browser. Linux Apache Tomcat. Any web browser. Windows CiderPress. Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Mac Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Linux Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS.",
    "CiderPress can convert DO disk images to other disk image formats, including:.PO - ProDOS Order Apple II Disk Image.2MG - Apple IIGS Disk Image NIB - Unadorned Nibble SDK - ShrinkIt Disk Archive APP - TrackStar Disk Image HDV - Sim //e Virtual Hard Drive DDD - DDD Pro Image."],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".AFM",
    "AFF Metadata File",
    "2.0",
    "ASCII text-based font format developed by Adobe; stores font metric data for a Type 1 PostScript file; contains the master design of a specific font, which defines the way each character of the font looks. Mac Referenced by programs that use system fonts Windows Referenced by programs that use system fonts. Metadata file created in the Advanced Forensics Format (AFF), a file format used to save disk image data for digital evidence; saves only the metadata that was extracted out of an.AFF disk image file; allows the metadata to be separated from the raw disk image data. AFM files reference the AFF disk image data. When opened, the supporting program automatically locates the disk image data in the separate files and loads it appropriately. Windows AFFLIB —  (Discontinued). PassMark OSForensics. Linux AFFLIB —  (Discontinued).",
    "Mac Referenced by programs that use system fonts. Windows Referenced by programs that use system fonts. Windows AFFLIB —  (Discontinued). PassMark OSForensics. Linux AFFLIB —  (Discontinued).",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".PO",
    "ProDOS Order Apple II Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "A PO file is a text-based object file used in software development. It may be referenced by Java programs, GNU gettext, or other software programs as a properties file. Most often, PO files contain user interface text translations that are referenced by GNU gettext. PO file open in GitHub Atom GNU gettext is a toolkit that helps programmers and translators produce user interface translation files. Most of these files are saved as PO files (others are saved as.POT files). Each PO file includes one or more entries, which associate an original, untranslated string(s) with its corresponding translation(s). The original text follows the identifier msgid; the translation follows the identifier msgstr. All translations in a single PO file are written in the same translation language. If a program requires multiple languages worth of translations, it will use multiple PO files. You can open and edit a PO file in any text or source code editor, such as Microsoft Visual Studio Code (cross-platform) or GitHub Atom (cross-platform). Windows GNU gettext. Referenced by various programs Edit with a text editor Mac GNU gettext. Referenced by various programs Edit with a text editor Linux GNU gettext. Referenced by various programs Edit with a text editor Web POFile.net. PO EditorFree. A PO file is a disk image created for use with an Apple II computer. It contains applications, games, and/or other files that can be loaded in an Apple II computer or emulator. PO files are saved using ProDOS sector order, rather than DOS 3.3 sector order. PO file open in CiderPress Apple released the first Apple II computer in June 1977. It was followed by the Apple II+, Apple II3, Apple IIc, and Apple IIGS. Several tools allow users to create images of these computers hard disks, to preserve the applications, files, and other data stored on them. These disk images are saved in many formats, including the PO format. Notably, PO format disk images save data in ProDOS sector order, while.DO disk images save data in DOS 3.3 sector order. (Some Apple II computers used the ProDOS operating system, while others used DOS 3.3.) Both formats may instead use the.DSK extension. When a user mounts a PO disk image in an Apple II computer or emulator, they can access the folders and files that disk image contains. Users can also examine a PO files contents in Apple II disk image utilities. You can open a PO file in several Apple II disk image utilities and Apple II emulators. For example: CiderPress (Windows) allows you to open a PO file to examine, extract, or add to its contents Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS (multiplatform) allows you to transfer PO files to an Apple II computer. KEGS (Kents Emulated GS) (multiplatform) allows you to open and mount PO files. Windows CiderPress. Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Mac Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Linux Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGSFree.",
    "Windows GNU gettext. Referenced by various programs Edit with a text editor. Mac GNU gettext. Referenced by various programs Edit with a text editor. Linux GNU gettext. Referenced by various programs Edit with a text editor. Web POFile.net. PO Editor. Windows CiderPress. Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Mac Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS. Linux Apple Disk Transfer ProDOS. KEGS.",
    "CiderPress can convert PO disk images to other disk image formats, including:.DO - DOS 3.3 Order Apple II Disk Image.2MG - Apple IIGS Disk Image NIB - Unadorned Nibble SDK - ShrinkIt Disk Archive APP - TrackStar Disk Image HDV - Sim //e Virtual Hard Drive DDD - DDD Pro Image."],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".QED",
    "QEMU Enhanced Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "A QED file is a disk image file saved in the QEMU Enhanced Disk Image (QED) format, which is a format used by QEMU virtualization software. It stores the hard drive contents of a QEMU virtual machine. The QED format is designed to improve upon the performance of the QEMU Copy On Write Version 2 (.QCOW2) format, which replaced the QEMU Copy On Write (.QCOW) format. However, the development of the format ceased and QEMU reverted back to the QCOW2 format. Windows QEMU. Linux QEMUFree.",
    "Windows QEMU. Linux QEMU.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".D81",
    "Commodore 64 1581 Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "A D81 file contains a disk image dumped from a 1581 floppy disk for Commodore 64 (C64), a popular personal computer from the 1980s. It stores information about a Commodore 64 game or program in D81 format, organized into 80 tracks holding 40 data sectors each. Various C64 emulators support D81 files, allowing users to run their stored game or program on a modern computer. D81 file open in VICE 3 The now-discontinued Commodore 64 supported various storage devices for transferring data. Supported devices include floppy disks, magnetic tape datasettes, and cartridges. Commodore 64 enthusiasts dump the game or program data from these devices and save them in different file formats (depending on the device from which they were dumped) to later run with an emulator. D81 is one of many formats utilized to store Commodore 64 games or programs. Other formats created by Commodore 64 enthusiasts include.D64,.G41,.T64,.D71, and.X64. You can open D81 files with various emulators, including Vice (multiplatform), WinVICE (Windows), and CCS64 (Windows). Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Mac VICE. Linux VICEFree.",
    "Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Mac VICE. Linux VICE.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".D71",
    "Commodore 64 1571 Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "A D71 file contains a disk image dumped from a 1571 floppy disk for Commodore 64 (C64), a popular personal computer from the 1980s. It stores information about a Commodore 64 game or program in D71 format, consisting of 256-byte data sectors organized into multiple tracks. Various C64 emulators support D71 files, allowing users to run their stored game or program on a modern computer. D71 file open in VICE 3 The now-discontinued Commodore 64 supported various storage devices for transferring data. Supported devices include floppy disks, magnetic tape datasettes, and cartridges. Commodore computing enthusiasts acquire actual Commodore 64 1571 floppy disks and save their data as a file in the D71 format. They can then load the D71 files in a Commodore emulator to run the game or program. Some users also upload the D71 files online to share them with other Commodore 64 enthusiasts. More information about the D71 format The D71 format supports data dumped from 1571 floppy disks, which may be one-sided like 1541 floppy disks (stored in the.D64 format), or double-sided. One-sided floppy disks typically store multiple 256-byte sectors in 35 tracks. Double-sided floppy disks store 70 tracks, resulting in a file doubled in size. D71 is one of several formats utilized to store Commodore 64 games or programs that computer enthusiasts load in emulators to run the game or program. Other formats created by Commodore 64 enthusiasts include D64,.G41,.T64, and.X64. You can open D71 files with various emulators, including Vice (multiplatform), WinVICE (Windows), and CCS64 (Windows). Mac VICE. Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Linux VICEFree.",
    "Mac VICE. Windows WinVICE. CCS64. Linux VICE.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VHDPMEM",
    "Hyper-V Virtual Machine Persistent Memory Device",
    "2.0",
    "A VHDPMEM file is a persistent memory device created by Microsoft Hyper-V, a Windows virtualization program. It can be attached to a virtual machine to add persistent memory to that machine. Hyper-V allows users to run multiple operating systems as virtual machines in Windows. It also allows users to create and manage virtualized software and hardware components. A VHDPMEM file is a virtualized hardware component. Specifically, a VHDPMEM file contains a virtual persistent memory (aka storage class memory or NVDIMM) device. Hyper-V users create VHDPMEM files using the New-VHD cmdlet. During the creation process, the user can specify the devices size. NOTE: The Windows Server 2016 and higher and Windows 10 and higher versions of Hyper-V support VHDPMEM files. VHDPEM files are not meant to be opened. To attach a VHDPEM file to a Hyper-V virtual machine, use the Add=VMHardDiskDrive cmdlet. Windows Microsoft Hyper-VFree.",
    "Windows Microsoft Hyper-V.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".FD",
    "IBochs Floppy Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "File used by LaTeX, a typesetting system used to create formatted documents; contains a font declaration that maps LaTeX font properties to TeX font specifications; used to describe how LaTeX font attributes are translated into TeX fonts. FD files are stored in a plain text format and contain commands that declare font families, font provider files, and font encodings. Mac LyX/Mac. AUCTEX Windows LaTeXEditor Lyx. JLatexEditor AUCTEX Linux Lyx. JLatexEditor. Data file used by DataFlex, a 4GL (Fourth-Generation Programming Language) used for creating business database applications; contains references to an internal DataFlex number as well as DataFlex column definitions. FD files are used by the DataFlex compiler to associate program code references with database tables. Windows Data Access Visual DataFlex. An FD file is a disk image used by the IBochs PC Emulator for Android. It contains a copy of a floppy disks contents, which can be loaded in IBochs. IBochs comes with one included FD file, named FREEDOS.FD. IBochs is an Android port of the Bochs PC emulator. It allows users to emulate various operating systems on their Android devices. To load an operating system or other data, users can save that data as an FD file. Users can then load the data using IBochs virtual floppy disk drive. For example, IBochs uses FREEDOS.FD to load the FreeDOS operating system upon initial startup. After using an FD file to load data, users can write that data to an IBochs.HD file. NOTE: Users can rename other floppy disk image files (such as.IMG files) to use the.fd extension to use them with IBochs. You can open an FD file with IBochs PC Emulator (Android). If you are using an FD file to boot an operating system: Make sure your FD file is stored in ~/​Android/​data/​lb.myapp.lbochs/​files. Open IBochs and press F12 to access the boot menu. Select 1. Floppy. IBochs will load the operating system your FD file contains. Android IBochs PC EmulatorFree.",
    "Mac LyX/Mac. AUCTEX. Windows LaTeXEditor Lyx. JLatexEditor AUCTEX. Linux Lyx. JLatexEditor. Windows Data Access Visual DataFlex. Android IBochs PC Emulator.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".RAW",
    "Fedora Linux Disk Image",
    "2.0",
    "A RAW file is an image generated by digital cameras such as Panasonic, Leica, and Casio cameras. It contains uncompressed, raw image data captured by a digital cameras CCD. Professional photographers often work with RAW files since they can more accurately edit images stored in the format. RAW file open in Adobe Photoshop 2021 RAW files store higher-quality images than more common image files, like.JPG and.PNG, because they do not lose data like JPG and PNG files do when they are compressed. However, because RAW files are not compressed, their file sizes are often very large. This is the tradeoff between lower-quality, smaller images and higher-quality, larger images. RAW files are one of many raw camera image formats. Some other common formats are Digital Negative Images (.DNG files), Canon Raw Images (.CR2 files), Sony Digital Camera Images (.ARW files), Kodak RAW Images (.DCR files), Nikon RAW Images (.NRW files), and Nikon Electronic Format Images (.NEF files). NOTE: RAW files may also be created by image generation software instead of digital cameras, although this is not as common. You can open RAW files with various image-editing and -viewing applications. Some options include Adobe Photoshop (Windows and macOS), Adobe Photoshop Elements (Windows and macOS), Corel PaintShop Pro (Windows), ACD Systems ACDSee (Windows), and RawTherapee (multiplatform). Web File Helper. Windows File Viewer Plus. Adobe Photoshop 2023. Adobe Photoshop Elements. Corel PaintShop Pro 2023. ACD Systems ACDSee Photo Studio. Canvas GFX Canvas X Pro. RawTherapee. Microsoft Photos Included with OS. GIMP with UFRaw plug-in. Mac Apple Preview Included with OS. Adobe Photoshop 2023. Adobe Photoshop Elements. GIMP with UFRaw plug-in. MacPhun ColorStrokes. WidsMob PhotoVault. Linux RawTherapee. digiKam. UFRaw. GIMP with UFRaw plug-in. Android File Viewer for Android. A RAW file is a virtual SD card created for use with the Dolphin video game console emulator. It contains data that would typically be stored on a Nintendo Wiis SD card, such as saved games. RAW files are typically named sd.raw. Dolphin is a multiplatform program that allows gamers to play Nintendo GameCube and Wii video games on their PCs. It allows players to load game- and console-related data from, and save data to, many different types of files, including RAW files. Each RAW file acts as a virtual Nintendo Wii SD card. Dolphin can load data from and save data to this file, just as a real Wii would load data from and save data to an SD card. For example, players can load saved games from an sd.raw file. They also can load data required by certain homebrew applications, which need an SD card (and data stored on that SD card) to function. How to create an SD.RAW file Gamers can use Dolphin itself or the Virtual SD Card Maker utility (multiplatform) to create an sd.raw file. For complete instructions, refer to Dolphins documentation. sd.raw - The typical name assigned to Dolphin virtual SD cards. To mount an sd.raw file in Dolphin (multiplatform): Open Dolphin and select Options → Configuration → Wii. In the SD Card Settings section, click the.. button that appears next to SD Card Path:. Navigate to and select your sd.raw file. You can also use Dolphin to edit an sd.raw files contents as though the file were an actual SD card. For more information, refer to Dolphins documentation. Windows Dolphin. Mac Dolphin. Linux DolphinFree. A RAW file contains uncompressed digital audio created using Pulse-Code Modulation (PCM). This raw data is saved in a binary format. RAW files do not contain a file header or use an audio codec. Because RAW files do not contain a file header, you may have trouble playing them in some media players. These media players require metadata that would typically be stored in an audio files header to play audio correctly. Thus, they cannot play RAW files. Many more-common types of audio files, including.WAV and.AIFF files, contain audio data created using Pulse-Code Modulation. However, these files contain a file header and use a recognized audio container format, allowing most popular media players to play them correctly. You can open a RAW file with Audacity (multiplatform), MPlayer (multiplatform), or Adobe Audition (Windows, Mac). Windows Audacity. Adobe Audition 2023. MPlayer. FMJ-Software Awave Audio. NCH Switch. NCH WavePad. Mac Audacity. Adobe Audition 2023. MPlayer. NCH Switch. NCH WavePad. Linux Audacity. MPlayerFree. A RAW file is a disk image used to install a Fedora Linux operating system. It contains a raw, binary copy of the operating systems data. RAW files are typically named for the operating system they are used to install and the architecture of the machine on which they are meant to be installed. Fedora Linux is a popular Linux distribution. Users can install various versions of the operating system on PCs, servers, and other devices. Users install Fedora Linux using disk images that contain a raw copy of the operating system. These images are saved in many formats, including.ISO,.QCOW2, and RAW. RAW files filenames include the operating system they are used to install and the architecture on which they are meant to be installed. For example, a RAW file named Fedora-Server-25-1.2.aarch64.raw contains a copy of the Fedora Server operating system that is meant to be installed on an AArch64 machine. You can use a RAW disk image to install the Fedora Linux operating system it contains on an appropriate device. To do so, boot your device from a USB or other drive containing the RAW disk image. All Platforms Boot your device from a drive containing the RAW file to install Fedora Linux. A RAW file is a data file created by an X-ray diffractometer. It contains the results of an X-ray diffraction analysis, used to determine a materials composition and structure. Several companies X-ray diffractometers save results as RAW files. An X-ray diffractometer shoots X-rays at a material and measures the resulting scattering pattern to determine the materials structure. Various scientists, including material scientists and structural biologists, use diffractometers to analyze materials composition. After performing a diffractometer analysis, scientists can often save the results of that analysis as a file. Several models of X-ray diffractometers, including devices produced by Siemens, Bruker, DiffracPlus, STOE, and MAC Science, save analysis results as RAW files. Scientists can use various programs and utilities to open these files, analyze the results they contain (usually shown as a spectrum and graph), and convert them to other formats. You can open RAW files with ICDD JADE Pattern Converter (Web), PowDLL (Windows), ConvX (Windows), MacDiff (Mac), and other X-ray diffractometer results analysis and conversion utilities. Note that, because different diffractometers save results differently, these programs may be able to open some types of RAW files but not others. You can use ICDD JADE Pattern Converter (Web), PowDLL (Windows), ConvX (Windows), and MacDiff (Mac) to convert RAW files to other X-ray diffractometer results formats and, often, the.TXT format. Windows PowDLL. ConvX. Mac MacDiff. Web ICDD JADE Pattern ConverterFree.",
    "Web File Helper. Windows File Viewer Plus. Adobe Photoshop 2023. Adobe Photoshop Elements. Corel PaintShop Pro 2023. ACD Systems ACDSee Photo Studio. Canvas GFX Canvas X Pro. RawTherapee. Microsoft Photos Included with OS. GIMP with UFRaw plug-in. Mac Apple Preview Included with OS. Adobe Photoshop 2023. Adobe Photoshop Elements. GIMP with UFRaw plug-in. MacPhun ColorStrokes. WidsMob PhotoVault. Linux RawTherapee. digiKam. UFRaw. GIMP with UFRaw plug-in. Android File Viewer for Android. Windows Dolphin. Mac Dolphin. Linux Dolphin. Windows Audacity. Adobe Audition 2023. MPlayer. FMJ-Software Awave Audio. NCH Switch. NCH WavePad. Mac Audacity. Adobe Audition 2023. MPlayer. NCH Switch. NCH WavePad. Linux Audacity. MPlayer. All Platforms Boot your device from a drive containing the RAW file to install Fedora Linux. Windows PowDLL. ConvX. Mac MacDiff. Web ICDD JADE Pattern Converter.",
    "Many image editors, including Adobe Photoshop and Corel PaintShop Pro, can convert RAW files to other formats. For example, Photoshop can convert RAW files to the following formats (and several others):.PNG - Portable Network Graphic.JPEG - JPEG Image.GIF - Graphical Interchange Format.PSD - Adobe Photoshop Document.BMP - Bitmap Image.PCX - Paintbrush Bitmap Image.TGA - Targa Graphic.TIFF - Tagged Image File Format. Audacity, Adobe Audition, and FMJ-Software Awave Studio can convert RAW files to other audio formats. For example, Audacity can convert RAW files to the following formats:.AIFF - Audio Interchange File Format.WAV - WAVE Audio.OGG - Ogg Vorbis Audio.FLAC -. Lossless Audio Codec.MP2 - MP2 Audio.M4A - MPEG-4 Audio.AC3 - Audio Codec 3.AMR - Adaptive Multi-rate Codec.OPUS - Opus Audio.WMA - Windows Media Audio."],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".DAO",
    "Disk-At-Once CD or DVD Image",
    "1.9",
    "A DAO file is a disk image that contains a copy of the content stored on a CD or DVD. It is used to write the content it contains to recordable CDs or DVDs, to create duplicate or backup copies. Various CD and DVD duplication and burning software can create and use DAO files. Disk-at-once, or DAO, is a method of writing data to a CD-R or DVD-R. While other CD and DVD writing methods write only portions of data (often, individual audio tracks) at once, DAO writes all data to a disc at the same time. This allows the person creating the disc to control whether there are gaps in the discs data and, if so, what data those gaps contain. For example, using the DAO method, users can insert a secret audio track at the start of another track, or they can link tracks together with no audio gap at all. Several CD and DVD authoring programs that can write discs using the DAO method save disk images as or can load DAO files. The data these disk images contain is used to produce duplicate DAO CD-Rs or DVD-Rs, which contain the same content as the DAO file. You can open a DAO file with Smart Projects IsoBuster (Windows) or EZB Systems UltraISO (Windows), to view the data it contains. UltraISO also allows you to burn the data your DAO file contains to a CD-R or DVD-R. Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster. EZB Systems UltraISO.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VC6",
    "Virtual CD 6 CD Image",
    "1.8",
    "Disc image format used by Virtual CD 6, a disc authoring application; specific to version 6 of the software only and contains data for a disc; used for storing the contents of CDs on the hard drive. NOTE: While VC6 files are specific to Virtual CD 6, Virtual CDs.VC4 format is compatible with Virtual CD 4 and higher. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD.",
    "Windows H+H Software Virtual CD.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".VC8",
    "Virtual CD 8 Disc Image",
    "1.8",
    "Disc image created by Virtual CD 8 disc authoring software; specific to version 8 of the software and stores disc data; enables the raw contents of a disc to be stored as an exact copy on the hard disk. NOTE: While VC8 files are specific to Virtual CD 8, Virtual CDs.VC4 format is compatible with Virtual CD 4 and higher. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD.",
    "Windows H+H Software Virtual CD.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".EDS",
    "Ensoniq SQ-80 Disk Image",
    "1.7",
    "An EDS file is a disk image formatted for an Ensoniq SQ-80 synthesizer, a digital workstation released by Ensoniq in 1987. It contains data dumped from a floppy disk, which includes sequences and effects. SQ-80 users would use floppy disks to add sequences and effects to the synthesizer, but users dump the data from the disks to EDS files to play their sounds. Ensoniq produced the SQ-80 from 1987 to 1989. The synthesizer followed the ESQ-1, which was Ensoniqs first synthesizer. The SQ-80 featured an improved sequencer, more sounds and instruments, and a floppy drive for sharing sound data via floppy disks. You can open an EDS file with Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools in Windows. Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "Windows Chicken Systems Ensoniq Disk Tools.",
    "Ensoniq Disk Tools can convert EDS files to various formats, including the following file types:.WAV - WAVE Audio File.MIDI - MIDI Music Data.SMF - Standard MIDI File.AIFF - Audio Interchange File Format."],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".D88",
    "Toshiba Pasopia 7 Disk File",
    "1.7",
    "Disk image saved in the Toshiba Pasopia 7 format; can be created from a Teledisk archive (.TD0 file) using the t2d file conversion program; may be opened by eQC-10, an open-source Epson QC-10/QX-10 emulator for Windows. The D88 file format is an uncompressed floppy disk image format that uses sector sizes of 128, 258, 512, or 1,024 bytes. Each sector header includes markers for track, side, sector, size, number of sectors, and density. Windows eQC-10.",
    "Windows eQC-10.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".XMF",
    "GameJack Disc Image File",
    "1.7",
    "Three-dimensional model mesh file created with Cal3D, an open source character animation library; contains polygons made up of nodes and edges, which comprise the geometry of the character; gives the model its skeletal structure. Cal3D meshes are more commonly stored using the binary.CMF format. NOTE: Cal3D models are used for applications such as IMVU, a 3D online chatting application that uses XMF files for virtual avatars. Windows Cal3dViewer. Cal3d2ogre. IMVU. Mac IMVU. Linux Cal3DFree. Family of music-related file formats created and administered by the MIDI Manufacturers Association; designed to contain multiple file types in a single file, such as MIDI data (.MID) files, instrument (.DLS) files, and wave audio (.WAV) files. Windows Beatnik Mobile Sound Builder Nokia Audio Suite 2.0. Disc image file originally created by GameJack, a program used for backing up game CDs and DVDs; similar to an.XMD file and stores an exact copy of a game disc; enables games to be backed up onto a hard drive and mounted with. GameJack, a German product, was replaced by Engelmann Media 5-Star GameCopy. The XMF format is now supported by additional disc imaging software programs such as HH Software Virtual CD. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. EZB Systems UltraISO. Disc4You GameJack.",
    "Windows Cal3dViewer. Cal3d2ogre. IMVU. Mac IMVU. Linux Cal3D. Windows Beatnik Mobile Sound Builder Nokia Audio Suite 2.0. Windows H+H Software Virtual CD. EZB Systems UltraISO. Disc4You GameJack.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".UFS",
    "FreeBSD Unix File System File",
    "1.5",
    "Unix file system file created for PC-BSD, a Unix operating system based on FreeBSD; stores the entire disk or a portion of a disk, including files, directories, and the file system structure; used to clone file systems for PC-BSD network installations. UFS files may be compressed into.UZIP files that use the multiple file extension.UFS.UZIP. Linux iXsystems PC-BSD.",
    "Linux iXsystems PC-BSD.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".AA",
    "Generic CD Image",
    "1.5",
    "Digital audio format used by Audible to store audio books; designed for storing spoken word audio and supports chapter markers, bookmarks, etc. Audible files are supported by the Apple iPod, Creative Zen, SanDisk Sansa, various GPS devices, and the Amazon Kindle. NOTE: AA audio files can be converted to the more compatible.MP3 format using a program like Tunebite, TuneCab, or SoundTaxi Media Suite. Windows File Viewer Plus. Audible AudibleManager —  (Discontinued). Apple iTunes —  (Discontinued). Nero 2022. SoundTaxi Media Suite. TuneCab. CoolUtils Total Audio Converter. Mac Apple iTunes —  (Discontinued). Generic disc image created by a disc imaging program; stores an exact replica of a hard disk or other type of media; basically an.ISO file with a different file extension. Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. Audible AudibleManager —  (Discontinued). Apple iTunes —  (Discontinued). Nero 2022. SoundTaxi Media Suite. TuneCab. CoolUtils Total Audio Converter. Mac Apple iTunes —  (Discontinued). Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "?"],
    ["disk_image_files",
    ".IBDAT",
    "IsoBuster Data File",
    "1.5",
    "An IBDAT file is a data file created by Smart Projects IsoBuster, a program used to recover data from damaged disks and file systems. It contains data blocks of a larger drive that are referenced by location paths stored in an.IBADR file. IBDAT files are created with IBADR files by IsoBuster to debug issues encountered by the program. The IBDAT file only stores the blocks of data from a large drive that are necessary for debugging an issue. This allows small disk image files to be created for much larger drives. NOTE: IBDAT files are extremely rare since they are only used for debugging purposes. Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "Windows Smart Projects IsoBuster.",
    "?"],
